{"qid": "2wikimqa:34896de8d6a2c70e09b6dc3d113475a58899961d2aa356b3", "question": "Who is the paternal grandfather of Bruno Ii Von Berg?", "answers": ["Adolf II of Berg"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, I can confirm one step of the chain: Bruno II von Berg (c. 1100 – 1137), Archbishop of Cologne from 1131 to 1137, was a son of **Count Adolf III of Berg**[2][3].\n\nHowever, the knowledge base does not contain any information about Adolf III of Berg's own father — i.e., Bruno II's paternal grandfather. Since no bridging evidence for that second hop was found, I cannot provide a verified name and must say that I do not know who Bruno II von Berg's paternal grandfather was based on the available sources.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 109704.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:78d3bf98f7b05622b428940d273b8c493f24a4468d1998dd", "question": "Are the movies The Hatton Garden Job and Carry On Cleo, from the same country?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes — both films are from the same country: the United Kingdom.\n\n- **The Hatton Garden Job** (also known as *One Last Heist*) is explicitly described as a 2017 **British** crime film[8].\n- **Carry On Cleo** was filmed at Pinewood Studios in Buckinghamshire and on Chobham Common in Surrey[1][11], indicating it is a **British** production as well.\n\nSo yes, they share the same country of origin.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 92048.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:9b0b29e391c61c570b20fae813486bc757c1fb7d25d83049", "question": "Who is the paternal grandfather of Dibyasambandh?", "answers": ["Mongkut"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, I can trace one step of the lineage but cannot fully confirm the answer:\n\n- Princess Dibyasambanna (Dibyasambandh) was a daughter of **Prince Bhanurangsi Savangwongse** and Mom Liam, born at Buraphaphirom Palace[3][4].\n- However, the knowledge base contains no record of who Prince Bhanurangsi Savangwongse's own father was, so the identity of Dibyasambandh's paternal grandfather cannot be verified from the available evidence.\n\nI therefore cannot give a confirmed name for her paternal grandfather based on this knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "entity_relation_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 82376.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:bdb42f1c30e16bbcdf6f0ab7c1f76765ddaa5817ee5e674b", "question": "Who is Charles Willoughby, 10Th Baron Willoughby Of Parham's paternal grandmother?", "answers": ["Lady Frances Manners"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Lady Margaret Clinton.**\n\nBased on the retrieved evidence, the reasoning chain is as follows:\n\n- Charles Willoughby, 10th Baron Willoughby of Parham (d. 1679) belonged to the same family line as his cousin and successor Thomas Willoughby, 11th Baron[4].\n- Thomas, 11th Baron was the grandson of Charles Willoughby, 2nd Baron Willoughby of Parham and his wife **Lady Margaret Clinton**[1][5].\n- Since the 10th Baron descended from this same paternal line of the 2nd Baron, his paternal grandmother was Lady Margaret Clinton, the wife of Charles, 2nd Baron Willoughby of Parham.\n\nNote: the knowledge base did not contain a document directly naming the 10th Baron's parents, so this conclusion is derived from the documented succession and family relationships rather than an explicit statement.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 174135.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:ca72ce55dc8f774617c794681dd7062eadfd14e679418cfa", "question": "What is the place of birth of Sextus Barbour's father?", "answers": ["Gordonsville, Virginia"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Sextus Barbour's father was Philip P. Barbour (Philip Pendleton Barbour, May 25, 1783 – February 25, 1841), a U.S. Congressman from Virginia who later served as an Associate Justice of the U.S. Supreme Court[2].\n\nBased on the retrieved evidence, Sextus Barbour was the sixth child of Philip P. Barbour, and the family lived in Orange County, Virginia, where Sextus himself was born in 1813[1][2]. While the retrieved snippets do not explicitly state the father's exact birthplace, Philip Pendleton Barbour is historically recorded as having been born at \"Frascati,\" his family's plantation near Gordonsville in **Orange County, Virginia** — the same county where his son Sextus was later born.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 59076.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:a7b5c5d84d974a849b0d042c435bbb3fe90ff50fd5d73f79", "question": "Where does the director of film L'Arbre, Le Maire Et La Médiathèque work at?", "answers": ["Cahiers du cinéma"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the director of *L'Arbre, le maire et la médiathèque* (1993) is Éric Rohmer, who wrote and directed the film[3]. \n\nHowever, while the knowledge base contains sections on Éric Rohmer's career as a journalist and his film career[7][6], the retrieved content does not include explicit details stating where he works. Therefore, I can confirm he is the director of the film, but I cannot verify from the available evidence the specific place of employment.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 306424.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:bbaa4eb6b32bad24fc2b8639d03be8c16d1140ec8454b16a", "question": "Who is the father of the director of film A No-Hit No-Run Summer?", "answers": ["Félix Leclerc"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n1. **Director**: The film *A No-Hit No-Run Summer* (French: *Un été sans point ni coup sûr*) was directed by **Francis Leclerc**[2].\n\n2. **Father**: Francis Leclerc is the son of **Félix Leclerc**, the legendary Québec singer-songwriter, poet, and actor.\n\nSo, the father of the director of *A No-Hit No-Run Summer* is **Félix Leclerc**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 186868.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:88e4806d6c6553000e3c1ff8be5c90f5309342954ce4acae", "question": "What is the date of death of Maria Isabel De Alcântara, Countess Of Iguaçu's mother?", "answers": ["November 3, 1867"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- Maria Isabel de Alcântara Bourbon, Countess of Iguaçu (28 February 1830 – 5 September 1896), was the third daughter of Emperor Pedro I of Brazil and his mistress **Domitila de Castro, Marchioness of Santos** [5]. So her mother was Domitila de Castro, Marchioness of Santos.\n\nUnfortunately, the retrieved documents do not explicitly state the Marchioness of Santos's date of death (the \"5 September 1896\" date appearing in the results refers to Maria Isabel herself, who died at sixty-six years of age in São Paulo [2][5]), so I cannot confirm her mother's exact death date from the knowledge base evidence available.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 69558.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:bc596d2ebcd25e79ca9cd689e50746e42e9a6fc579afc683", "question": "Who is Henri Ii, Duke Of Nemours's paternal grandfather?", "answers": ["Jacques"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Henri II, Duke of Nemours's paternal grandfather was **Jacques of Savoy** (Jacques, Duke of Nemours).\n\nReasoning chain from the retrieved evidence:\n- Henri II, Duke of Nemours was the third son of Henri de Savoie, 4th Duc de Nemours (Henri I, Duke of Nemours) [3].\n- Henri I, Duke of Nemours (Henri of Savoy) was the son of Jacques of Savoy and Anna d'Este [2].\n\nTherefore, through his father Henri I, Henri II's paternal grandfather was Jacques of Savoy.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 173412.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:f73db1873c311b2a38f27a52c5e05b021da0c2fe9590dac3", "question": "Who is Gisela Of Hungary's father-in-law?", "answers": ["Géza"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, Gisela of Hungary married **King Stephen I of Hungary** in 996[2][3]. Therefore her father-in-law was Stephen I's father — **Géza, Grand Prince of the Hungarians**.\n\nNote: the knowledge base chunks confirm the marriage itself[2][3], though they do not explicitly name Stephen's father; Géza is supplied from general historical knowledge of Stephen I's lineage.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 178280.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:3cff7074281c58717aa65021d875cc1e6a6321f71cd77742", "question": "When did Frances Vane, Marchioness Of Londonderry's father die?", "answers": ["1 August 1813"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Frances Vane (Frances Anne), Marchioness of Londonderry, was the daughter of **Sir Henry Vane-Tempest, 2nd Baronet**, and he died in **1813**[4]. Upon his death, Frances Anne inherited extensive lands in northeast England and property in County Antrim, Ireland[4].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 106867.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:53f6acc70a61443a93c25c91ca7dc7f8780046d02e28c94a", "question": "Who is the paternal grandmother of King Kang Of Zhou?", "answers": ["Yi Jiang"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **King Kang of Zhou** (personal name Ji Zhao) was the son of **King Cheng of Zhou**[4], which is also confirmed by the relation lookup (\"King Cheng of Zhou — father of — Crown Prince Zhao, ruled as King Kang of Zhou\").\n\nTherefore, King Kang's paternal grandmother would be the **mother of King Cheng of Zhou**. However, the retrieved documents do not contain any information identifying who King Cheng's mother was, so I cannot confirm the name of King Kang's paternal grandmother from the available evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 236363.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:0c35123885c26fc474a7cd8e7c03fe75bfff4fc15845d3b9", "question": "Were Jake Eisenhart and Michael T. Ullman of the same nationality?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes — Jake Eisenhart and Michael T. Ullman were both American.\n\n- **Jake Eisenhart** was an American left-handed pitcher in Major League Baseball, appearing in one game for the Cincinnati Reds in 1944; he was a native of Perkasie, Pennsylvania[1].\n- **Michael T. Ullman** (born July 29, 1962, San Francisco, California) is an American neuroscientist known for his Declarative/Procedural model of language[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 46797.5, "error": ""}
