{"qid": "hotpotqa:9729ff4cf6a13382c88896cf5a5b176d082cc1137c66b38b", "question": "What city, located in Lincoln County, Montana, United States, is Vegepet based in?", "answers": ["Troy, Montana"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Vegepet is based in **Troy, Montana** — a city in Lincoln County, Montana, United States[13]. Vegepet is a line of dietary supplement products for dogs and cats being fed a vegan diet, sold by Compassion Circle[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 379395.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:b785963089bd7f81ee294751c688af85bfe2863dba623373", "question": "Who choreographed one who is said to be the last muse for the choreographer and starred in the Nutcracker?", "answers": ["George Balanchine"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**George Balanchine** is the choreographer in question.\n\nThe reasoning chain:\n1. The dancer described as the \"last muse\" for a famous choreographer is **Darci Kistler**, widely regarded as George Balanchine's last muse.\n2. She starred in *The Nutcracker* — specifically Balanchine's version, including the 1993 film adaptation (*George Balanchine's The Nutcracker*).\n3. That production's choreography was by **George Balanchine**, as reflected in the evidence showing \"George Balanchine's The Nutcracker\" associated with the Balanchine Trust[2][3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 328969.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:ed6c85899d2a29f6dc8e02ba62bc8f1b1043a8c135fdd132", "question": "For what type of work is the production company for The Year Without a Santa Claus best known?", "answers": ["seasonal television specials, particularly its work in stop motion animation"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**The Year Without a Santa Claus** was produced by **Rankin/Bass Productions**, founded by Arthur Rankin Jr. and Jules Bass[1][8].\n\nThe studio is best known for its **stop-motion and animated holiday television specials** — particularly classic Christmas programs such as *Rudolph the Red-Nosed Reindeer* — which made it synonymous with seasonal TV programming[1]. Its signature technique, often called \"Animagic,\" used stop-motion puppet animation, and the studio built a filmography largely around holiday-themed works[3][5].\n\nIn short: Rankin/Bass is best known for producing stop-motion animated Christmas/holiday television specials.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 70385.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:4c34b33d7a545c4eba3848d54fc4568fdf186e849897aa6e", "question": "Which three-time Olivier Award winning English actress starred in the 1981 BBC television serial, Sons and Lovers?", "answers": ["Dame Eileen June Atkins"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Eileen Atkins** is the answer. The 1981 BBC television serial *Sons and Lovers* — based on D. H. Lawrence's 1913 novel — starred Eileen Atkins, Tom Bell, Karl Johnson, Lynn Dearth and Leonie Mellinger[7], and Eileen Atkins is the English actress who has won three Olivier Awards.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 52063.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:74ae5bfc8bdac7b560ec92b71604e86654f87b8f7fcbb30a", "question": "The historical Nimavar school in the Nimavar Bazaar, or bazar, is located in which country?", "answers": ["Iran"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The Nimavar school is located in **Iran**. It is a historical school in Isfahan, situated in the Nimavar Bazaar. Built in 1691 during the Safavid era under Suleiman I, it is found among the historic buildings of the Qeysarie bazaar in the Grand Bazaar of Isfahan[1][4].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 67199.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:75f3798af3596cffefe8b932a00b1976f3f25fa0f42089fc", "question": "Which name adopted by African-American activist Allen Donaldson co-found Black Power movement of the 1960s and 1970s", "answers": ["Hakim Abdullah Jamal"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Allen Donaldson adopted the name **Hakim Jamal** after converting to Islam, and as Hakim Jamal he became an African-American activist associated with the Black Power movement of the 1960s and 1970s[1][3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 97297.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:207efa55e96afdf705e48bc57b58fc8232bb972a47073d50", "question": "Which film was released first, Summer Magic or Around the World in 80 Days?", "answers": ["Summer Magic"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Around the World in 80 Days** was released first.\n\n- *Around the World in 80 Days* — released in **1956**[7][10]\n- *Summer Magic* — a Walt Disney Productions family musical, released in **1963**[1]\n\nSo the 1956 film predates Summer Magic by seven years.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 373140.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:1a71b5a73ffb21cb52d41e09b1f861748b60f1e59273a080", "question": "Arnold Richards was the former chair of what organization that is a member of the Center for Jewish History?", "answers": ["YIVO"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**YIVO Institute for Jewish Research.**\n\nArnold Richards（美国精神分析学家）曾担任 YIVO Institute for Jewish Research 的前主席。该机构是 Center for Jewish History（犹太历史中心）的五个成员组织之一，其余成员包括 American Jewish Historical Society、American Sephardi Federation、Leo Baeck Institute New York 和 Yeshiva University Museum[6]。", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 99024.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:99635fd2d7a227cb3360253cf0f5759ed87bbac33593d830", "question": "Which mountain is higher, Tongshanjiabu or Himalchuli?", "answers": ["Himalchuli has three main peaks: East (7893 m), West (7540 m) and North (7371 m)"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Himalchuli is higher.\n\n- **Tongshanjiabu** stands at **7,207 metres** (23,645 ft) tall [2].\n- **Himalchuli**'s highest peak, its East summit, reaches **7,893 metres** (with West and North peaks at 7,540 m and 7,371 m respectively) [6].\n\nSo Himalchuli (7,893 m) exceeds Tongshanjiabu (7,207 m) by roughly 686 metres.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 45917.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:76096cce081ab5b9ef72be19076ee0a79d9e9239474e8b73", "question": "When did the place in Canada where François Beaucourt (1740-1794) was mainly active acquired its present name?", "answers": ["1763"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**1763.**\n\nFrançois Beaucourt (1740–1794), the first native-born Canadian painter to study in France with a professional reputation, was active mainly in the **Province of Quebec**[1]. That place acquired its present name in **1763**: following the Royal Proclamation of 1763, after Britain conquered New France in the Seven Years' War, Canada was renamed the Province of Quebec[5].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 179114.6, "error": ""}
