{"qid": "hotpotqa:f1c0b9a9ec47ccda8ba4b1be0ca7083b961ba0f06b2a4df7", "question": "What Nobel Laureate teaches at  Chapman University?", "answers": ["Vernon Smith"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on my knowledge, **Vernon L. Smith** is the Nobel Laureate associated with Chapman University. He won the 2002 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for his pioneering work in experimental economics and joined Chapman University as a professor of economics, helping establish its Economic Science Institute.\n\nPlease note: the retrieved knowledge base results only returned document titles (e.g., \"Notable people,\" \"Accomplishments\") [2][3] without the underlying text content, so I could not verify the specific detail from the source documents themselves — the answer above is drawn from general knowledge rather than confirmed by the retrieved chunks.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 356745.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c47c8d3b1917f017cafaecbb4cddd6b92171854247957c08", "question": "Who was born first,  Morgan Llywelyn or Robert Jordan?", "answers": ["Morgan Llywelyn"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Morgan Llywelyn was born first. She was born on **December 3, 1937**[1], while Robert Jordan (James Oliver Rigney Jr.) was born on **October 17, 1948**[7] — about 11 years later.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 42281.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:f536646dd72a8a4f5ebddec61a67b8319dc9187d47bb7fc6", "question": "The winner of the the London Riders' Championship in 1953 scored how many points in the 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship?", "answers": ["14"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- The **London Riders' Championship in 1953** was won by **Jack Young**, the Australian speedway rider who also won the Speedway World Championship in 1951 and 1952[5].\n\nHowever, the retrieved documents do not contain Jack Young's exact points total from the 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship final, so I cannot confirm the precise number from the knowledge base alone. External records generally list him as scoring **14 points** in that 1952 Wembley final, but this figure is not verified by the retrieved evidence here.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 142256.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:6e541b3b8fc7e59da0da6548a17932b403b2e865d4036982", "question": "A wide receiver for the Toronto Argonauts played college football for a team that represents the University of Oklahoma, which belongs to what conference?", "answers": ["Big 12 Conference"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base navigation, the relevant wide receiver connected to the Toronto Argonauts is **Justin Brown** (listed among the KB's wide-receiver athlete profiles alongside Argonauts players like Arland Bruce III and Todd Lowber)[1]. Brown played college football for the **Oklahoma Sooners**, the team representing the University of Oklahoma, which competes in the **Big 12 Conference**.\n\nPlease note: my evidence searches surfaced details on Argonauts receivers such as Arland Bruce III (who won the 2004 Grey Cup with Toronto)[2] and Todd Lowber (who had multiple stints with the team)[3], but the search limit was reached before I could retrieve a chunk directly verifying Brown's Oklahoma/Big 12 linkage — so while Big 12 is the well-established conference affiliation for Oklahoma, that specific hop is based on general knowledge rather than a cited KB passage here.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 173128.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:6eca7d7affce3375ec084f6356c6fc8ddf75eda0f6a4382f", "question": "In which six Western European territories have Celtic languages or cultural traits survived?", "answers": ["Brittany, Cornwall, Ireland, Isle of Man, Scotland and Wales."], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The six territories are **Brittany (Breizh), Cornwall (Kernow), Ireland (Éire), the Isle of Man (Mannin), Scotland (Alba), and Wales (Cymru)** — the regions widely considered the \"Celtic nations,\" where Celtic languages and cultural traits have survived in Northwestern Europe[1][2].\n\nIn each, a Celtic language is still spoken to some extent:\n- **Brittonic (Brythonic) languages**: Breton in Brittany, Cornish in Cornwall, Welsh in Wales\n- **Goidelic (Gaelic) languages**: Scottish Gaelic in Scotland, Irish in Ireland, Manx on the Isle of Man[1]\n\n(Note: parts of north-western Iberia — Galicia, Asturias, León, Cantabria, northern Portugal — are sometimes considered culturally Celtic too, but no Celtic language is attested there in modern times, so they are not counted among the six[1].)", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 166710.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:8bcda9c521bb7cb5dcd9a8d4987bd5bcf4ec455a84c62f81", "question": "Which is farther north, Steel Venom or Wicked Twister?", "answers": ["Wicked Twister"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Steel Venom is farther north.**\n\nAccording to the knowledge base:\n- **Steel Venom** is located at Valleyfair amusement park in **Shakopee, Minnesota**, United States[1].\n- **Wicked Twister** was located at Cedar Point amusement park in **Sandusky, Ohio**, United States[8].\n\nSince Shakopee, Minnesota lies north of Sandusky, Ohio, Steel Venom sits farther north than Wicked Twister.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 36997.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:1d81e1f410acc77f1d9a844f811045c2cecb0fdc9e717d41", "question": "Which filmmaker was known for animation, Lev Yilmaz or Pamela B. Green?", "answers": ["Levni Yilmaz"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Lev Yilmaz** was known for animation. He is an American independent filmmaker and artist best known for his \"Tales of Mere Existence\" animated comic series[2].\n\nBy contrast, Pamela B. Green is known for her work in feature film titles and motion graphics, as well as directing the documentary *Be Natural: The Untold Story of Alice Guy-Blaché*[10] — not animation per se.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 43621.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:77a3188a3b97619e1661cc289bb1a951308223219592696b", "question": "Who was the gunman of the hostage crisis which Chris Reason was awarded the Graham Perkin Australian Journalist of the Year Award for his coverage of?", "answers": ["Man Haron"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Chris Reason won the Graham Perkin Australian Journalist of the Year Award for his coverage of the **Lindt Cafe siege** in Sydney in December 2014[1][2]. The gunman in that siege was **Man Haron Monis**, an Iranian-born self-styled cleric who took hostages at the Lindt Chocolate Café in Martin Place, Sydney, on 15–16 December 2014.\n\nSo, the gunman was **Man Haron Monis**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 57969.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:5e7675388334946fc508c4c8ac4ab0c491910fcc0e048bfd", "question": "SR connector is based on the connector that is often used for what type of cable?", "answers": ["coaxial"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The SR connector is based on the American **BNC connector**, which is commonly used for **coaxial cable** — the SR connector itself is described as a Russian-made RF connector for coaxial cables, differing from the BNC only slightly in dimensions due to imperial-to-metric conversion discrepancies[3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 164230.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:3e4cce5082e968aec7f0c008b1acbd111f505d0579d1c9ee", "question": "Why did the CEO of the football team based in Denver, Colorado step down in 2014?", "answers": ["due to the onset and progression of Alzheimer's disease"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The CEO in question was **Pat Bowlen**, who served as CEO and majority owner of the Denver Broncos — the NFL team based in Denver, Colorado. He stepped down in July 2014 because of the onset and progression of **Alzheimer's disease**[5]. On July 23, 2014, he officially relinquished control of the team to team president Joe Ellis, amid his battle with the disease[4][1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 28788.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:9dc6d234e2406aef691ea02ddc2064f343fc30cbc534c937", "question": "Are Pothos and Tibouchina Aubl both flowering genus of plants?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes. Both are flowering plant genera:\n\n- **Pothos** is a genus of flowering plants in the family Araceae (tribe Potheae), native to China, the Indian Subcontinent, Australia, New Guinea, Southeast Asia, and various Pacific and Indian Ocean islands.[1]\n- **Tibouchina** (established by Aublet in 1775, hence the \"Aubl.\" author abbreviation) is a neotropical flowering plant genus in the family Melastomataceae, with species that are subshrubs, shrubs or small trees typically bearing purple flowers.[7][10]", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 27750.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:11b4c60bf00735e20ae817b940d86aa8922e2fcdaa3a0b47", "question": "Which is in the pop genre, Capital Cities or Tweaker?", "answers": ["Capital Cities"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Tweaker** is described as an American **alternative rock** collaboration project founded by Chris Vrenna, whose style incorporates synthpop, progressive rock, modern jazz and electronica[8] — so it's generally classified as alternative rock rather than straight pop.\n- The knowledge base did not return an explicit genre listing for **Capital Cities**, but Capital Cities is the act of the two commonly categorized as a **pop** band (an American pop/indie pop duo), whereas Tweaker is not primarily a pop act.\n\nSo the answer is **Capital Cities** — it's the one in the pop genre, while Tweaker leans toward alternative rock with electronic elements.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 421177.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:7bb5645d62fc946b46910757e007f59e87f52bbf8535da58", "question": "The Pluralist school is said to have included what creator of the theory of atomism? ", "answers": ["Leucippus"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The Pluralist school is said to have included **Leucippus** — traditionally credited as the originator of the theory of atomism — along with his pupil **Democritus**, who together developed the atomic theory[1][2]. They lived in Abdera, Thrace, and are most famous for their atomic cosmology, which held that atoms move within the void and interact mechanically to form the plurality of the world[2].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 52101.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:73d978e7e37a9c7475daa9151f9ead2bd3042e72988cafdd", "question": "What type of structure does 277 Park Avenue and 126 Madison Avenue have in common?", "answers": ["building"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Both 277 Park Avenue and 126 Madison Avenue are **skyscrapers** in New York City.\n\n- **126 Madison Avenue** (also known as Madison House / 15 East 30th Street) is explicitly described as a residential skyscraper — 47 stories and 730 feet (220 m) tall — located in NoMad, Manhattan[8].\n- **277 Park Avenue** is likewise associated with the \"List of tallest buildings in New York City\"[3], indicating that it is also a tall skyscraper structure.\n\nSo the structure they have in common is that both are skyscrapers (high-rise/tall buildings) in New York City.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 40933.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:21849142d27a4c8410bf8e8042f016054a0a0493e0e8b6e6", "question": "Which City in the Miami metropolitan area is home to the Primetime Race Group?", "answers": ["Hollywood, Florida"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Hollywood, Florida. The knowledge base indicates the Primetime Race Group is a privateer motorsport team based in Hollywood, Florida, which competes in the American Le Mans Series and the IMSA Lites Series[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 92451.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:81d43196cac0effe1645b207f826a30550efc3126c35371e", "question": "In what event was Harold Davis a former record holder, but now is held by Usain Bolt?", "answers": ["100 metres"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base, **Harold Davis** (1921–2007) was an American sprinter and a former world record holder[1]. The event in question is the **100 metres (100 m)** — a record he once held, which is now held by Usain Bolt.\n\nNote: the retrieved passages confirm Davis's status as a former world-record-holding sprinter but do not explicitly restate the event name; the connection to the 100 m comes from combining his sprinter profile[1] with the fact that Bolt's current records are in the sprints, most notably the 100 m.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 135677.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:f5ca9a0360773900f848072d3d7c63034922bdd0e6925e64", "question": "Operation light house was an experiment by Wernher von Braun and another man born in what year?", "answers": ["1895"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Operation Lighthouse was the failed experimental launch of four Aggregate 3 liquid-fuel rockets carried out by Wernher von Braun together with **Walter Dornberger** on the German island of Greifswalder Oie in December 1937[1][2]. Walter Dornberger was born in **1895**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 107014.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:4aee23759e842807b156492356b920a11236c7ea3da20732", "question": "Are Werther and The Odyssey both operas?", "answers": ["no"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "No — only one of them is.\n\n- **Werther** is an opera: specifically a *drame lyrique* in four acts by Jules Massenet, to a French libretto by Édouard Blau, Paul Milliet, and Georges Hartmann, loosely based on Goethe's novel *The Sorrows of Young Werther* [1][2].\n- **The Odyssey** is not an opera. It is Homer's ancient epic poem. However, operas have been based on it — for example, Claudio Monteverdi's *Il ritorno d'Ulisse in patria* (first performed in 1640), which draws on the second half of the Odyssey [7].\n\nSo while both works have inspired operas, only Werther is itself an opera; the Odyssey is an epic poem.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 61035.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:6d8f9be94b67277442c42aa9b437edaf901efec0d0959bda", "question": "Are both Duke Energy and Affiliated Managers Group based in Massachusetts?", "answers": ["no"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "No — they are not both based in Massachusetts:\n\n- **Duke Energy** is an American electric power and natural gas holding company headquartered in **Charlotte, North Carolina**, not Massachusetts [1][4].\n- **Affiliated Managers Group (AMG)** does have a Massachusetts presence — it was founded in Boston and lists Prides Crossing, Massachusetts among its principal offices [8] — but its other principal offices are in West Palm Beach, Florida; Stamford, Connecticut; and London, so it is not based solely in Massachusetts either [8].\n\nSo the answer to whether both are based in Massachusetts is no: Duke Energy is a North Carolina company, while AMG's headquarters presence spans multiple locations including, but not limited to, Massachusetts.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 32558.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:b7af427ee230063a2aabb35691c7a81670c0b7e85ac3b7b2", "question": "How many marchers were in the protest that Pelham D. Glassford ended? ", "answers": ["43,000 marchers"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the protest Pelham D. Glassford ended was the **1932 Bonus Army protest** — he was \"in large measure held responsible for the violence that ended the 1932 Bonus Army protests\"[1].\n\nHowever, the retrieved evidence does not state the specific number of marchers in that protest, so I cannot confirm the exact figure from the knowledge base. (It is commonly reported elsewhere as roughly 43,000 marchers including veterans and their families, but this is not verified by the retrieved documents.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 59029.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e7875d33cdf7138818681e7bb2a13310bba28ea94550d64f", "question": "Which conference is the team for which Justin Bannan played college football currently a member of?", "answers": ["Pac-12 Conference"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Justin Bannan played his college football at the University of Colorado[2]. The team he played for — the Colorado Buffaloes — is currently a member of the **Big 12 Conference** (they rejoined the Big 12 in 2024 after leaving the Pac-12).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 185375.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:666b2401378aceb59c9a4d6cc75b006f7c6af8a00c7d6f19", "question": "Where is the airline company that operated United Express Flight 3411 on April 9, 2017 on behalf of United Express headquartered?", "answers": ["Indianapolis, Indiana"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the airline connected to the United Express Flight 3411 incident was **Republic Airline**, one of the carriers under contract to operate United Express flights on behalf of United[2].\n\nRepublic Airline is headquartered in **Indianapolis, Indiana, USA**. Please note, however, that the retrieved knowledge base excerpts confirm Republic Airline's role as the United Express contract carrier involved in the incident[2], but did not contain an explicit statement of its headquarters location — that detail comes from general knowledge rather than the cited evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 67249.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:2bfda75e23447ca7e3213d01331c31816f30190a2ff6df71", "question": "Bill Bryson grew up in a city that is the seat of which county ?", "answers": ["Polk County"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Bill Bryson grew up in **Des Moines, Iowa** — his memoir *The Life and Times of the Thunderbolt Kid* recounts his youth there during the 1950s and early 1960s[4], and *The Lost Continent* begins his journey in his childhood hometown of Des Moines[1].\n\nDes Moines is the county seat of **Polk County**, Iowa. (Note: this county-seat detail was not directly confirmed by the retrieved evidence; it reflects general knowledge.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 118412.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:a2bb6c8de2a57c51b35e9d5c005cf46e1c0457f4086ef77d", "question": "What was the first year a scientific journal published by an organization located in the Bronx was published?", "answers": ["1909"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- The organization located in the Bronx that publishes a scientific journal is the **New York Botanical Garden (NYBG)**, situated at Bronx Park in the Bronx, New York City[1].\n\nThe scientific journal it publishes is **Brittonia**, which was first published in **1931**.\n\n**Answer: 1931**\n\n(Note: the knowledge base results confirm the NYBG's location in the Bronx[1], but the retrieval round limit was reached before I could directly retrieve the chunk verifying Brittonia's founding year of 1931, so that detail is based on general reference knowledge rather than a retrieved citation.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 203535.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:60ec93a22fbcb65eeadd9f1d2b3c8e4ed44d3a2c4285b168", "question": "What song from the fourth studio album made by The Who reached No.4 on the UK charts and No. 19 o nthe U.S. \"Billboard\" Hot 100?", "answers": ["Tommy"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**\"Pinball Wizard.\"**\n\nIt is a song written by Pete Townshend and performed by The Who, featured on their 1969 rock opera album *Tommy* (the band's fourth studio album). Released as a single in 1969, it reached **No. 4 on the UK charts and No. 19 on the U.S. Billboard Hot 100**, matching the chart positions in your question[3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 48088.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:203709148e7052e14bf493f95037458b606b42f2fff54c79", "question": "Brigadier Stanley James Ledger Hill was attached to the command post of which senior British Army officer born on July 10, 1886?", "answers": ["Lord Gort"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Viscount Gort (Field Marshal John Standish Surtees Prendergast Vereker, 6th Viscount Gort)** — the senior British Army officer born on 10 July 1886, best known as Commander‑in‑Chief of the British Expeditionary Force in 1939–40.\n\nThe retrieved evidence confirms the first hop of the chain: Brigadier Stanley James Ledger Hill (14 March 1911 – 16 March 2006) was a British Army officer who commanded the 3rd Parachute Brigade of the 6th Airborne Division during the Second World War[1]. The senior officer whose command post Hill was attached to matches the birth date given in your question — John Vereker, 6th Viscount Gort, born 10 July 1886.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 191051.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:9336554e511e88766228ffa1acd1c6f82c9a41046514c253", "question": "Who did Fredric Rieders tesify agains who had killed as many as 60 patients and recieved three consecutive life terms to be served in Florence Colorado?", "answers": ["Michael Swango"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Fredric Rieders testified against **Michael Swango**, also known as \"Dr. Death.\" Swango was a serial killer physician who killed as many as 60 of his patients at various hospitals he worked at, using succinylcholine and epinephrine[2]. He was sentenced in 2000 to three consecutive life terms without the possibility of parole and is serving his sentence at ADX Florence (Colorado) at his own request[8].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 21735.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:a93aa642fb8802a99b36cd62febe1c4e995096a9320b00c5", "question": "Henry Roth and Lewis Mumford are both this nationality?", "answers": ["American"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Both Henry Roth and Lewis Mumford were **American**.\n\n- **Lewis Mumford** is explicitly described as \"an American historian, sociologist, philosopher of technology, and literary critic\"[7].\n- **Henry Roth**, though born in Tysmenitz, Galicia (Austro-Hungary), arrived at Ellis Island and began his life in New York in 1908[14], and is known as a chronicler of Jewish-American immigrant experience in New York City[4].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 35981.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:f1ea70c4e2c13d2fda1feda75a72a345c0e91f083220e95a", "question": "Who was the last monarch of England to be overthrown before The English General Election, 1690?", "answers": ["King James II of England"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**James II** was the last monarch of England to be overthrown before the English general election of 1690.\n\nReasoning chain:\n- The 1690 general election followed the political upheaval of 1688–89; indeed, the preceding 1689 election returned the Convention Parliament, which was summoned \"in the aftermath of the Glorious Revolution\" [1].\n- The Glorious Revolution was the event in which James II was deposed — he was replaced by William III and Mary II, making him the last English monarch to be overthrown.\n\nSo, prior to the 1690 election, the last monarch of England to have been overthrown was **James II**, during the Glorious Revolution of 1688.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 99628.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:fbd2e6218ec7c8fc1a90b05bf6858a15d376704a278fa79f", "question": "Who was born first, Juan Rulfo or John le Carré?", "answers": ["Juan Rulfo"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Juan Rulfo was born first.**\n\n- **Juan Rulfo** was born in **1917** in Apulco, Jalisco, Mexico [1] (though he often gave 1918 to enter the military academy).\n- **John le Carré** (real name David John Moore Cornwell) was born on **19 October 1931** in Poole, Dorset, England [8].\n\nThat makes Juan Rulfo about 14 years older than John le Carré.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 29695.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:d18fde9d0b8da2a2febb67a679847ee4ee3259e85a9565f4", "question": "What year was the author of Cock and Bull born? ", "answers": ["1961"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The novel *Cock and Bull* was written by **Will Self** — it's a volume of two novellas originally published by Bloomsbury in 1992[1][2].\n\nWill Self was born in **1961** (26 September 1961). Note: while the knowledge base confirms Self as the author[1][2], its retrieved passages did not explicitly state his birth year, so 1961 comes from general background knowledge rather than a cited source.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 31302.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:0aaabfd136117761896b5931d7b468ff8c61909e00140470", "question": "Where is the company that created Doravirine ( from?", "answers": ["American"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, **Doravirine** (sold under the brand name Pifeltro) was developed by **Merck & Co.**, an American pharmaceutical company[1]. Merck & Co. is headquartered in **Rahway, New Jersey, United States** — so the company behind Doravirine is from the USA.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 38789.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:bcd98af30c2eb7ce1233459715d27f428b111d12fea529b4", "question": "Angelspit toured the United States with an American electronic music duo from Orlando, Florida, that formed in what year?", "answers": ["2006"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on my searches, I was unable to find the information needed to answer this question. The retrieved evidence covers other artists (e.g., Deftones' members [1][2][5], Capital Cities [3], and The Hundred in the Hands, whose debut album is described as being by an American electronic music duo released in 2010 [4]), but none of it identifies the American electronic music duo from Orlando, Florida that Angelspit toured the United States with, nor that duo's formation year.\n\nI therefore cannot confirm the formation year without inventing facts, so I have to say I don't know the answer based on the available knowledge base evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 169423.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:5affd6b287c8ce97502760e33a71598e8eee24b78c3dd95c", "question": "On May 25, 2017 Greg Gianforte won the special election following the resignation of a politican that current holds what position?", "answers": ["United States Secretary of the Interior"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Greg Gianforte won the May 25, 2017 special election for Montana's at‑large congressional seat, which was vacated when **Ryan Zinke** resigned from the House of Representatives to serve as **United States Secretary of the Interior** in the Trump administration.\n\nSo the politician who had resigned (Zinke) went on to hold the position of **U.S. Secretary of the Interior**. The retrieved knowledge base also corroborates Gianforte's subsequent career path — he did not seek reelection to the House in 2020 and instead won the 2020 Montana gubernatorial election, becoming governor[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 123416.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:795ed5382ff9378f90123aedc2b638c049411295c5678466", "question": "Are Tantrix and Personal Preference both types of games?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes, both are games:\n\n- **Personal Preference** is explicitly documented as a 1987 board game created by Donal Carlston, in which players guess the order in which another player prefers foods, activities, people, and other items; it was published by Broderbund in the US, Playtoy Industries in Canada, and Parker Brothers International in Britain[6].\n- **Tantrix** is documented through entries covering its gameplay, notes, and tileset[1][2][4] — it is a tile-based puzzle game played with hexagonal tiles that players arrange to form continuous colored lines.\n\nSo while they are very different kinds of games (a party-style guessing board game vs. a strategy tile puzzle), both fall under the category of games.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 38988.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:38bd41e515abf2a44d29c593748db043ba2276d93938fdad", "question": "What rule-class city of 26,595 contains the neighborhood Plainview?", "answers": ["Jeffersontown, Kentucky"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Jeffersontown, Kentucky.** Plainview is a neighborhood in Jeffersontown, located on the former site of the Plainview Dairy Farm and developed as a Planned Unit Development[1]. Jeffersontown itself is a home rule-class city in Jefferson County, Kentucky, with a population of 26,595 at the 2010 U.S. census[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 82560.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:034b6ac41cd902b7fbde6b304eb2930d8eea616924ba461f", "question": "What public radio network in New York has allowed Eric Plakun to dis his psychiatric work?", "answers": ["WAMC"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Eric Plakun has discussed his psychiatric work on **WAMC**, the Albany, New York affiliate of NPR (National Public Radio)[1].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 26368.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:4d6aafffd109cb61fd543f44bda770fda0cf1e1c3798548b", "question": "1956 Night Series Cup games were played at what inner suburb of Melbourne that is 3km south of Melbourne's central business district?", "answers": ["Albert Park"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**South Melbourne.**\n\n- The 1956 Night Series Cup (VFL Night Premiership Cup) games were played at **Lake Oval**, the home ground of **South Melbourne**, as it was the only ground equipped to host night games[2].\n- South Melbourne fits the description given — an inner suburb of Melbourne located roughly 3 km south of the city's central business district.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 162862.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e54d1e07a1fc2ce2afdf7e75f79561d8fe0abd22f4d8bd53", "question": "Who directed the film where Susanne Pollatschek voiced Olivia Flaversham?", "answers": ["Burny Mattinson, David Michener, and the team of John Musker and Ron Clements"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\nSusanne Pollatschek voiced Olivia Flaversham in **The Great Mouse Detective**[1][2], the 1986 animated film set in London where Olivia seeks Basil of Baker Street's help to find her kidnapped father[3][4]. Regarding direction, the evidence identifies **Ron Clements** as a co-director of the film — noting that \"Val Bettin was co-director Ron Clements's first choice for Dawson\"[5], and that Pollatschek herself was selected for the role of Olivia over hundreds of other applicants during that production[5].\n\nSo the film is *The Great Mouse Detective*, and Ron Clements is confirmed in the knowledge base as one of its co-directors.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 111211.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:9303f959145f198df63d2d7ec604a9d6efcb3e8daca89e91", "question": "What types of activities occur in both the Bennington and Bridgewater Triangles?", "answers": ["alleged paranormal phenomena"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, both areas share similar characteristics as noted by author Joseph A. Citro[1]. The activities that occur in both include:\n\n1. **Unexplained disappearances / missing persons** – The Bennington Triangle denotes an area of southwestern Vermont where a number of people went missing between 1945 and 1950[1], and Citro stated that this area shares characteristics with the Bridgewater Triangle in Southeastern Massachusetts[1].\n\n2. **Paranormal and mysterious folklore phenomena** – Both triangles are treated as sites of strange lore: the Bennington Triangle's stories of vanished persons and local legends such as the \"Bennington Monster,\" a Bigfoot-like creature said to roam the woods, have been featured in paranormal TV programs like *Most Terrifying Places in America* and *William Shatner's Weird or What?* under themes of \"Mysterious Vanishings\" and an \"Unnatural World\"[4], reflecting the same kind of mystery-focused attention associated with these triangle regions.\n\nIn short, both locations are known for mysterious vanishings of people and surrounding paranormal folklore.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 44952.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:b4dfcf1c4f152ba5684531d0eaa43f33ec5b185b92a36884", "question": "Don Holmes played for the football club that is based in what eastern suburb of Perth?", "answers": ["Bassendean"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Bassendean** — Don Holmes (Donald Shane Holmes) played in the West Australian Football League for Swan Districts[1], and the Swan Districts Football Club is based at Bassendean Oval, in Bassendean, an eastern suburb of Perth, Western Australia[2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "entity_relation_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 140659.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:d7d92c85b8becc33e60fc03507933f8eaf53b5e1067286de", "question": "What is the name of this suburb within the Central Coast Council local government area, where Tessa, of Microwave Jenny, grew up?", "answers": ["Umina Beach, New South Wales"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Tessa grew up in **Umina Beach**, New South Wales. According to the knowledge base, Microwave Jenny's vocalist Tessa Nuku was born in Ulladulla, New South Wales, before moving to and growing up in Umina Beach[1] — a suburb located within the Central Coast Council local government area.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 86490.8, "error": ""}
