{"qid": "hotpotqa:22a4122731366b93dd7495f9cb64b9312537fbe85797efbc", "question": "Who stars in the title role of a Netflix show co-created by Robert Carlock?", "answers": ["Ellie Kemper"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base navigation, the Netflix show co-created by Robert Carlock is **Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt** (identified in the Entertainment and Media Works Cluster[3], which he co-created with Tina Fey). The title role of Kimmy Schmidt is starred by **Ellie Kemper**.\n\nNote: The direct evidence search did not return a document explicitly confirming Kemper's casting before the retrieval limit was reached, so while this aligns with well-known information about the show, the specific casting detail could not be verified against a retrieved source chunk.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 155855.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e5e18488f0e900206332a857f70da66dc59829937c913ff0", "question": "What form of play does Yameen and Activision have in common?", "answers": ["video game"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the common form of play is **video games**. The knowledge base identifies Activision as a game company with a catalog of \"Notable games published\"[6], while Yameen appears in the collection among entertainment media entries[2][5]. Both share the form of play known as video games.\n\nNote: the retrieved evidence was largely limited to section headings rather than full text, so I could not verify the precise details linking Yameen to video games beyond his classification within the entertainment media material.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 163331.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:54d1487358089d52613d8e6970c3efdd5c56d112159d2810", "question": "Camp Courtney was named after the Major killed in action at the battle codenamed what?", "answers": ["Operation Iceberg"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Operation Iceberg.**\n\nAccording to the knowledge base, Camp Courtney (a U.S. Marine base in Uruma City, Okinawa, Japan) is named after Major Henry A. Courtney, Jr., who was killed in action at the Battle of Okinawa[1] — the WWII battle that was codenamed Operation Iceberg.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 47164.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:2bf8384417cec2f1a2d43a2602326d90a97622d87898b1cc", "question": "What was the nationality and profession of the person responsible for the concept of a dimensionless number in physics and engineering?", "answers": ["French mathematician and physicist"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The person responsible for this concept is **Joseph Fourier**. He laid the groundwork for dimensionless quantities through his contributions to **dimensional analysis**, notably introducing the concept of *dimensional homogeneity* in equations — the idea that an equation can only be formally correct if the dimensions match on both sides[2].\n\nAccordingly:\n- **Nationality:** French — he was born in Auxerre, France, in 1768[10]\n- **Profession:** Mathematician and physicist — best known for initiating Fourier series/analysis and their applications to heat transfer and vibrations[10]", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 142453.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:5748ce1eca37fc55596e58fcff71e45363396002d28e0af9", "question": "Baraki Barak District is situated in the western part of a province whose capital is what?", "answers": ["Puli Alam"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Baraki Barak District is situated in the western part of **Logar Province**, Afghanistan[1]. The province's capital is **Puli Alam** — notably, the retrieved documents indicate that the district's own center, the town of Baraki Barak, was the *former* provincial capital[1][2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 37961.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:302ee593ca790dfb2dcbfd4341817fc64f8d106f65f61867", "question": "Who is a winner of the 2013 6 Hours of Silverstone a co-commentator for?", "answers": ["BBC Formula One"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the 2013 6 Hours of Silverstone was won by Briton Allan McNish, Dane Tom Kristensen, and Frenchman Loïc Duval driving for Audi[4]. Among these winners, the one who works as a co-commentator is **Allan McNish** — he serves as a co-commentator for **Eurovision/Eurosport's television coverage of the FIA World Endurance Championship** (the same series in which this race was the opening round)[4].\n\nNote: the retrieved chunks confirm the race winners and that the event opened the 2013 FIA World Endurance Championship season[4], while the detail about McNish's co-commentary role comes from background knowledge rather than the retrieved snippets.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 57461.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:ad26b1fdaa97e8ef1c290a41a9151cc6b453756cb2715176", "question": "What organization does Sandra Pizzarello and Doctorate have in common?", "answers": ["University"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, I can confirm Sandra Pizzarello's organizational affiliations: she was a project collaborator/co-investigator for the **NASA Astrobiology Institute (NAI)**, president of the **International Society for the Study of the Origin of Life** (2014–2017), and an emerita professor at **Arizona State University**[5]. She also held a Doctor of Biological Sciences degree (a doctorate) from the University of Padua[3][5].\n\nHowever, the knowledge base did not return any evidence identifying \"Doctorate\" as an entity with its own organizational affiliation, so I cannot reliably determine a specific organization that Sandra Pizzarello and \"Doctorate\" share beyond noting that Pizzarello herself held a doctorate degree.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 377439.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:b3647c02310a89ccb2822c163da3bead1ad3cd21948d4769", "question": "Where is the host of the American survival skills reality show, The Island, originally from?", "answers": ["Northern Ireland"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Host identification**: *The Island* is an American survival skills reality television series hosted by adventurer and survivalist **Bear Grylls** on NBC, which began airing May 25, 2015[1]. It is based on the British series *The Island with Bear Grylls*[3].\n\n- **Origin**: Unfortunately, the retrieved evidence does not contain any passage stating Bear Grylls' birthplace or where he is originally from, and I was unable to complete a second-hop verification before reaching the retrieval limit. So I can confirm who the host is, but I cannot verify from the knowledge base exactly where he is originally from.\n\n(For reference, general knowledge holds that Bear Grylls was born in Doncaster, England, but this specific detail is not supported by the retrieved documents here.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 71159.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:dc14a859291c1d678432626f6f7974f8ecca536f4b7bdd6c", "question": "Who was born later, Jerry Garcia or Joe Gooch?", "answers": ["Joe Gooch"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Joe Gooch was born later. Jerry Garcia was born on August 1, 1942[3], while Joe Gooch was born on May 3, 1977[6] — about 35 years after Garcia.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 93032.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:228572a285ddcbb4947f362c07531238d1eec58e0fe0cfc6", "question": "Where is the University Joe Odom played football for located?", "answers": ["West Lafayette, Indiana"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Joe Odom (Sammy Joe Odom) played college football for **Northwestern State University**, playing linebacker/defensive tackle for the Northwestern State Demons in the Gulf States Conference[1]. That university is located in **Natchitoches, Louisiana** (consistent with his Louisiana roots — he was born in Shreveport and attended high school in Minden, Louisiana[1]).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 48285.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:1650b91e6fb722111b6461fc9524295246c3bd3b1a342267", "question": "Roger Stuart Woolhouse is a biographer of a philosopher commonly known as what ?", "answers": ["Father of Liberalism"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Roger Stuart Woolhouse was a biographer of the philosopher **John Locke** — commonly known simply as **\"Locke\"**[1][2][3]. Woolhouse, an English philosopher and expert on empiricism and rationalism, wrote *Locke: A Biography* (Cambridge University Press, 2007), which Cambridge University Press commissioned as the first major biography of Locke since Maurice Cranston's 1957 work[1][2][3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 165521.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:88b095fb93df1d962625daa1796a46ce4721c316b0ab819b", "question": "The telenova \"El Ardiente Secreto\" was based ona novel published under what pen name?", "answers": ["\"Currer Bell\""], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "I found the first hop — the telenovela is based on Jane Eyre by Charlotte Brontë. Now let me verify the pen name used for that novel's publication.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 30181.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e9b828ee7169d1978f3bcb6de5bbb0f54f9ee3bfc0ffdb8b", "question": "National Firearms Agreement was in response to the Port Arthur massacre that killed how many people?", "answers": ["35 people"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The National Firearms Agreement was made in 1996 in response to the Port Arthur massacre, which killed **35 people**[1]. Following the massacre, Prime Minister John Howard led the development of strict gun control laws, and the Agreement—establishing heavy restrictions on automatic and semi-automatic weapons along with a gun buyback program and national registry—was announced within two weeks[2][3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 89505.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:ea58013717bdbe9f0bad1991ed03d1afe3208bf514d9a2cd", "question": "Le Rêve was created by the director behind what Cirque du Soleil production from 1998 onward?", "answers": ["O"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**O** — the Cirque du Soleil resident show that premiered in Las Vegas in 1998.\n\nReasoning chain:\n1. *Le Rêve* (the water-based show at Wynn Las Vegas) was created by director **Franco Dragone**[1–5].\n2. Dragone is the same director behind Cirque du Soleil productions such as *Mystère* (created under his direction)[7].\n3. From 1998 onward, his signature Cirque du Soleil production was **O**, staged at Bellagio in Las Vegas[6][8][10].\n\nSo *Le Rêve* was created by Franco Dragone, the director of Cirque du Soleil's **O** (running since 1998).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 369460.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:75d66ce7bb5fbcea2d942895c0b10144ba9a94282e2e9f15", "question": "What do Lavinia Greenlaw and Nâzım Hikmet have in common?", "answers": ["poet and novelist"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on my searches, I was unable to retrieve any document in the knowledge base that mentions **Lavinia Greenlaw** or establishes a direct connection between her and Nâzım Hikmet. The available material covers Nâzım Hikmet himself — his life, style and achievements[1][2], his family background[5] — and the International Nazim Hikmet Poetry Award named after him[3], but none of these pieces reference Greenlaw.\n\nWhat can be said with confidence from general knowledge is that both figures are known as **poets**: Nâzım Hikmet is celebrated as a major Turkish poet whose legacy includes an international poetry prize[2][3], and Lavinia Greenlaw is a British poet. However, since the knowledge base contains no evidence about Greenlaw or a specific shared attribute (such as a common award, nationality, or affiliation), I cannot verify any more concrete commonality between the two, and I do not want to invent one.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 424496.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e9348ea3d217789522ba17d5dd2c69c01bacc9d677c614a4", "question": "In what year did the Tornoto International Film Fesitval premiere a movie produced by BBC Films?", "answers": ["2011"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base, the BBC Films production in question is **Benediction** — it appears among the films listed under BBC Films[4]. *Benediction*, Terence Davies' biographical drama about war poet Siegfried Sassoon, premiered at the **Toronto International Film Festival in 2021**.\n\nSo the year was **2021**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 55803.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:781060824875cb0343a110f5e6f83d46e638cbb89e2ad7c6", "question": "The FIBT World Championships 1960 took place in a town located in which part of Italy ?", "answers": ["the Veneto region of Northern Italy"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The FIBT World Championships 1960 were held in **Cortina d'Ampezzo, Italy** for the fifth time (after hosting in 1937, 1939, 1950, and 1954)[1]. \n\nCortina d'Ampezzo is an alpine town situated in the **Dolomites mountain range of northeastern Italy**, in the Veneto region (province of Belluno).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 41228.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:690c31a6d6263dacd649fefc5b5cfa94210205fdcdb43905", "question": "Are both French Spaniel and Beagle universally recognized breeds?", "answers": ["no"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**No.** The two breeds are not both universally recognized:\n\n- **French Spaniel**: It is recognized by some kennel clubs — the Canadian Kennel Club (since 1985), the Fédération Cynologique Internationale, and the United Kennel Club — but **not** by The Kennel Club (UK) or the American Kennel Club[1]. The AKC has only placed it in its Foundation Stock Service, which is described as \"the first step to full recognition\"[3], and full AKC recognition typically requires a lengthy process including a national breed club and years in the Miscellaneous Class[1].\n\n- **Beagle**: The retrieved evidence did not provide explicit details on the Beagle's kennel club recognition status, though it is a long-established breed (the results only reference sections on its hunting use, popularity, and varieties)[6][7][8][10].\n\nRegardless of the Beagle's status, since the French Spaniel is explicitly not recognized by major clubs like the UK Kennel Club and AKC[1][3], the answer is that they are **not both universally recognized**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 113599.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:8e9e0bac1908657b2f48452fe70dd4f7ab765b9633b75627", "question": "When was the club formed, for which Adam  Johnson played as well as Middlesbrough and Watford ?", "answers": ["1919"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**Manchester City** – the evidence confirms that Adam Johnson, after coming through the Middlesbrough youth academy and making 120 appearances for them (plus loan spells at Leeds United and Watford), went on to play for Manchester City[3][5].\n\nManchester City was formed in **1880** (originally founded as St. Mark's (West Gorton) before adopting the name Manchester City).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 66368.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e8ba4badc3c5d30cc0942ede8df86a72257c492c07025dab", "question": "In modern history of Syria this kingdom lasted for only four months, this kingdom surrendered to who?", "answers": ["French"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The kingdom was the **Arab Kingdom of Syria**, which existed for a little over four months, from 8 March to 25 July 1920, under Faisal bin Hussein (son of Sharif Hussein bin Ali). It **surrendered to French forces on 25 July 1920**[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 94719.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:3acb0cc0a04a3f71078ee120e1b7ca4f089548b7cc8a8056", "question": "Were the board games, Clans and Drunter und Drüber, both created by Leo Colovini?", "answers": ["Drunter und Drüber is a multiplayer board game invented by Klaus Teuber"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "No. While the board game **Clans** was indeed designed by Leo Colovini, **Drunter und Drüber** was invented by a different designer — Klaus Teuber. It was first published in 1991 in Germany by Hans im Glück, with a second edition released in 1994[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 30207.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:3518c800877d0b8cc48ca575de745502b472a139564ffa46", "question": "3 Arts Entertainment had at least one coalition with what streaming service, with its 13-episode, 2015 season of a Tina Fey created sitcom?", "answers": ["Netflix"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Netflix.**\n\nThe evidence chain:\n- 3 Arts Entertainment produced the sitcom *Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt* among its TV shows[2].\n- *Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt* was created by Tina Fey (and Robert Carlock), and was originally set as a 13-episode first season on NBC for spring 2015 before being sold to Netflix, where it has streamed since March 6, 2015[1].\n\nSo the coalition/alliance of 3 Arts Entertainment was with Netflix for that show's 2015 season.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 80035.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:90caafc7fe51eee4143874971f67bb3e0107d26179c437f7", "question": "Broadbent Arena and Freedom Hall are landmarks of which U.S. city?", "answers": ["Louisville"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Louisville, Kentucky.** Broadbent Arena — a 6,600-seat multi-purpose arena — is located on the grounds of the Kentucky Exposition Center in Louisville, along with Cardinal Stadium and Freedom Hall [1]. Freedom Hall likewise serves as an indoor sporting and concert venue in the Kentuckiana area (Louisville) [5].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 37121.2, "error": ""}
