{"qid": "hotpotqa:de89026c970fbb6dff04176107b9cf20407d155bc1ae2eec", "question": "Where did the punter for the Dallas Cowboys in the 1980s play college football?", "answers": ["Arizona State University"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The punter for the Dallas Cowboys in the 1980s was **Jim Miller** (James Gordon Miller), who played his college football at the **University of Mississippi** (Ole Miss), where he earned All-American honors[2]. He punted in the NFL for eight seasons during the 1980s, playing professionally for the San Francisco 49ers, Dallas Cowboys, and New York Giants[2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 27507.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:e0873e8c51c3e473d614f6ff9705557ec2f37972ec4be718", "question": "In 135 BC, a second campaign was sent to intervene in a war between Minyue and another ancient kingdom established at the collapse of whom?", "answers": ["the Qin dynasty"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**The Qin dynasty.**\n\nIn 135 BC, war broke out when Minyue invaded Nanyue, and King Zhao Mo of Nanyue received Han military assistance to repel it[2]. Nanyue itself was an ancient kingdom established by Zhao Tuo — formerly Commander of Nanhai of the Qin Empire — in 204 BC, after the collapse of the **Qin dynasty**[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 99938.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:d3b2acfdec13f925424cd7d96b689557edfd08e6dd6177b7", "question": "Who won more awards, Brothers Quay or Jake Kasdan?", "answers": ["Brothers Quay"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Brothers Quay won more awards.**\n\n- **Brothers Quay**: They **won the 1998 Drama Desk Award** for Outstanding Set Design (Best Design) for their work on the play *The Chairs*, and were also nominated that year for a Tony Award[16][17].\n- **Jake Kasdan**: The knowledge base records no wins — only a **2008 Golden Globe nomination** for Best Original Song for *Walk Hard* (shared with John C. Reilly, Judd Apatow, and Marshall Crenshaw), which he lost to Eddie Vedder's \"Guaranteed\" from *Into the Wild*[19].\n\nSo with at least one actual award win versus zero recorded wins (only a nomination) for Kasdan, the Brothers Quay come out ahead.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 118868.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c5d6aa8cb0ad17a88eb66d578e86f783e3ac1f3684df9425", "question": "How long is the river The Atherton Bridge spans?", "answers": ["37.5"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved context, I could only confirm that an “Atherton Bridge” entry exists in the knowledge base (with sections like \"Technical information\" and a cross-reference to the Ponakin Bridge)[1][3][5], but the snippets did not contain the river name or any length figure, so I cannot cite verified evidence for this specific fact.\n\nFrom general knowledge (unverified against this KB): the Atherton Bridge is a historic covered bridge in Lancaster, Massachusetts, that crosses the **South Branch of the Nashua River**. The Nashua River itself is approximately **37 miles (about 60 km)** long. However, since the knowledge base did not return the underlying text confirming either the river or its length, please treat this as background information rather than a KB-verified answer.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 116941.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:31386c51d334a83befff8c1264657e197fad8b2885906939", "question": "Why is Bangor Daily News talkin about Sawin Millett?", "answers": ["Commissioner by the Maine Senate"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base, the Bangor Daily News comes up in connection with Sawin Millett because of a poll it conducted: **in December 2011, a poll of policymakers by the Bangor Daily News ranked Sawin Millett (Howard Sawin Millett Jr.) as the ninth most influential person in Maine politics**[2].\n\nFor context, Millett is a Maine Republican politician who served as Commissioner of Administrative and Financial Services under Governor Paul LePage from 2011 to 2014, and his appointment to that role was unanimously approved by the Maine Senate in January 2011 with backing from key Democrats[2]. So the newspaper's mention of him stems from his prominent standing in state government and its own influence ranking.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 25700.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:ce14f5dd15a5819d08a11f04e015e35a6f3640119973f806", "question": "Are Portland International Airport and Gerald R. Ford International Airport both located in Oregon?", "answers": ["no"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "No. According to the retrieved evidence, **Portland International Airport** is indeed located in the U.S. state of Oregon — it is the largest airport in Oregon, situated within Portland's city limits in Multnomah County[2].\n\nHowever, **Gerald R. Ford International Airport** is not located in Oregon. It is located in Grand Rapids, Michigan (named after former President Gerald R. Ford, who was from that area). The retrieved documents about this airport only contained section headings (History, Airlines and destinations, Facilities, etc.) without explicit location details, so I cannot cite a specific passage confirming its state from the knowledge base — but it is well established that it serves Grand Rapids, Michigan, not any city in Oregon.\n\nSo the two airports are not both in Oregon: only Portland International Airport is in Oregon.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 324039.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:b524574cca42824f963480aae00867719108113389d087d3", "question": "What river does Waterloo Bridge and London Eye have in common?", "answers": ["Thames"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**The River Thames.**\n\n- **Waterloo Bridge** is one of the crossings of the River Thames in London[6][7].\n- **The London Eye** stands on the South Bank of the Thames, served by the London Eye Pier on the river[11][12].\n\nSo the common feature shared by both landmarks is the River Thames.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 55724.5, "error": ""}
