{"qid": "musique:26bd6d08ea272ac11bebcf5250b16ff90f92352cdac405c1", "question": "What is the source of the river that is the mouth of the Caledon River?", "answers": ["Thaba Putsoa"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**Step 1 – Mouth of the Caledon River:** The Caledon River is part of the Upper Orange Water Management Area, listed alongside the Orange River as one of its major rivers[1][2]. This indicates the Caledon River flows into the **Orange River**, which serves as its mouth/recipient waterway. The Orange River document also confirms a direct geographic connection between the two rivers, noting that the Lesotho Highlands Water Project delivery tunnel passes under the Lesotho–South Africa border at the Caledon River[3].\n\n**Step 2 – Source of the Orange River:** Unfortunately, the retrieved documents do not explicitly state the source location of the Orange River — the available excerpts cover the Lesotho Highlands Water Project and river listings, but not the Orange River's headwaters. From general knowledge, the Orange River rises in the Lesotho Highlands (in the Maloti/Drakensberg mountain region), but I cannot verify this detail against the knowledge base evidence provided here.\n\nIn summary: the river receiving the Caledon River at its mouth is the **Orange River**[1][2][3], whose source lies in the highlands of Lesotho — though the latter point could not be confirmed from the retrieved documents.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 43951.6, "error": ""}
