{"qid": "musique:7d7eb390be7dc75ca61b5eda6a9d9b36372273d1bd42646a", "question": "Who gives out the prize named after the author of Lectures on Jurisprudence?", "answers": ["University of Cambridge", "Cambridge"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer:** The *Lectures on Jurisprudence* were written by **Adam Smith**, so the prize named after their author is the **Adam Smith Prize**. This prize is awarded by the **University of Cambridge** (for students of economics), as indicated by the knowledge base's topic entry linking the Adam Smith Prize with the University of Cambridge[3].\n\nNote: the direct evidence retrieval confirmed the existence of the work *Lectures on Jurisprudence*[1][2][4], while the attribution of the prize's awarding body comes from the topic map entry associating the Adam Smith Prize with the University of Cambridge[3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 173157.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:8fc8fb567703000eb6065c007e97841b11c9595b8862197b", "question": "Where did the producer of Julius Caesar study or work?", "answers": ["Clifton College"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\nThe producer of the 1953 film *Julius Caesar* was **John Houseman** — after a success with *The Bad and the Beautiful* (1952), \"He followed it with the film adaptation of Julius Caesar (1953)\" [entity_relation_lookup, chunk 01a01779-be82-749f-b087-dce6abc03704].\n\nAs for where he worked: **Houseman worked at MGM** — \"Houseman's stint at MGM began with *Holiday for Sinners* (1952)\" [same chunk]. The same source also notes he co-wrote Broadway plays earlier (*Three and One*, 1933; *And Be My Love*, 1934) [chunk 01a01779-ae8b-7da5-9835-cafa50235727].\n\nRegarding formal education (where he studied), the retrieved evidence does not contain this information, so I cannot confirm it from the knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 56459.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:1f4b2eebc578880d3f401476be8e933f743e77a865a31902", "question": "What is the name of the famous bridge located in the birthplace of the composer of Nulla in mundo pax sincera?", "answers": ["Rialto Bridge", "Ponte di Rialto"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Rialto Bridge.**\n\n- Hop 1: *Nulla in mundo pax sincera* (RV 630) is a sacred motet composed by **Antonio Vivaldi** in 1735[1].\n- Hop 2: Vivaldi's birthplace is Venice, and the famous bridge located there is the **Rialto Bridge**, as covered in the knowledge base's Venice/Rialto Bridge entries[6][7][8].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 165326.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:936a34800c73e9d077b058fda4576fc45f8cb03bfae99c04", "question": "What is the performer of Heartbeat named after?", "answers": ["Natalie Albino", "Nina Sky"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "\"Heartbeat\" is performed by the duo Nina Sky[1]. Their group name is named after the members themselves: the sisters took the first two syllables of their own names (\"Ni\" and \"Na\") to form \"Nina\", then added \"Sky\", which they felt represented \"independent twins\"[7].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 59889.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:606e28838e5641e3dab2482d21f65cf2915cacdf6d63b454", "question": "When did the party that gained control of Congress in the midterm elections in 1946 take control of the government branch that determines the rules of the US House?", "answers": ["January 2015"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer: January 3, 1947.**\n\nReasoning chain:\n\n1. **Which party?** In the 1946 midterm elections (during President Truman's first term), the **Republican Party** gained control of both chambers of Congress — a result covered by the knowledge base's materials on the 1946 U.S. House of Representatives elections[1].\n\n2. **Which branch?** Under Article I of the U.S. Constitution, each house of the **legislative branch (Congress)** determines the rules of its own proceedings — so the body that sets the rules of the U.S. House is Congress itself.\n\n3. **When did they take control?** The new Congress seated as a result of those elections — the **80th United States Congress** — convened on **January 3, 1947**, which is when the Republicans formally took control of Congress.\n\nNote: the retrieval system returned only state-level page fragments (e.g., Alabama, Arizona, Arkansas) from the 1946 election documents before hitting its search limit, so the specific convening date of January 3, 1947 reflects general historical knowledge rather than directly retrieved text from this knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 196292.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:8f9aa7e502ca3fcd999cb05b87a6aa78ef4c4af96cbe3199", "question": "What year did the publisher of Labyrinth end?", "answers": ["1986"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**1986.** Labyrinth (1984) was published by Acornsoft[1], which ceased to operate as a separate company upon the departure of David Johnson-Davies in January 1986. After that, Acorn Computers continued using the Acornsoft name on its VIEW-family office software, and Superior Software took over publishing some Acornsoft games under license[8].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 89424.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:865a174ebe32ad81f573feb122198c1f16e6f2fd6c3fdda5", "question": "The food safety system of the agency that has the authority to decide if a drug is marketed otc or prescription is known as?", "answers": ["Food Safety Modernization Act (FSMA)"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer: The FDA Food Safety Modernization Act (FSMA) framework.**\n\nReasoning chain:\n1. The agency with the authority to decide whether a drug is marketed as over-the-counter or prescription is the **U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)** — in the United States, the manufacture and sale of OTC substances are regulated by the FDA, which reviews drugs through New Drug Applications (NDAs) and the OTC monograph system[5].\n2. That agency's food safety system is known as the one established under the **FDA Food Safety Modernization Act (FSMA)**, signed into law in 2011, which modernized the U.S. food safety framework by shifting the focus from responding to contamination to preventing it, including preventive controls for food facilities, produce safety standards, and import safety programs.\n\nNote: the FSMA details above come from the knowledge base's topic navigation rather than fully verified evidence chunks, but the identification of the FDA as the deciding agency is directly supported by retrieved evidence[5].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 71824.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:177dde027032c5c71bf88fd4676536c0bef7d79d43c32659", "question": "Who is the president of the newly declared independent country that has a Commission of Truth and Friendship with the country where Ambelau is located?", "answers": ["Francisco Guterres"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, here is the reasoning chain:\n\n1. **Ambelau** is a volcanic island located in the Banda Sea within the Maluku Islands of **Indonesia**, part of South Buru Regency, Maluku province[1][2][3].\n\n2. The Commission of Truth and Friendship involving Indonesia is the **Indonesia–Timor Leste Commission of Truth and Friendship**[6][7][8] — meaning the other party is **Timor-Leste (East Timor)**, the country that newly declared (restored) its independence.\n\n3. As for the president of Timor-Leste: the retrieved passages only identified the commission itself and did not explicitly name the president. Based on general knowledge, the president of Timor-Leste is **José Ramos-Horta** (in office since May 2022; he also served as president 2007–2012, following independence-era presidents Xanana Gusmão, Taur Matan Ruak, and Francisco Guterres). Please treat the specific name with slight caution, as this detail came from background knowledge rather than the retrieved evidence above.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 91712.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:ecfec467704be2a08675527e8f1733d9fbb51309454145ef", "question": "Who is the president of the group Kim Un-yong is a member of?", "answers": ["Thomas Bach"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- Kim Un-yong was a **founding member of the WTF (World Taekwondo Federation) executive board**[1].\n- He himself served as President of the World Taekwondo Federation, but **retired from the WTF presidency in 2004 and was succeeded by Chungwon Choue**[1].\n\nSo the group Kim Un-yong belonged to is the **World Taekwondo Federation**, and its presidency after him was held by **Chungwon Choue** (Kim Un-yong had previously been its president from the organization's founding until his retirement in 2004)[1][3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 59510.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:e956e4868d588132bd2de1926dc446d0975c2d7a45090cad", "question": "The author of Elizabeth and After attended which university?", "answers": ["University of Toronto", "UT"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, *Elizabeth and After* is a novel by **Matt Cohen**, first published in 1999 by Knopf Canada[1]. However, while I located documents about Matt Cohen[6][7][8][9][10], the retrieved content did not include specific information about which university he attended, so I cannot confirm his university from the knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 228481.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:9724776e8c8210a47eae48302b73a9e2603c238d417a63f2", "question": "During the war in which The Things They Carried is set, when was conscription introduced by the country where the film Grievous Bodily Harm was later released?", "answers": ["1964"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer: 1964**\n\nReasoning chain:\n\n1. **The Things They Carried** is set during the **Vietnam War**, as it follows American soldiers fighting on the ground there[1].\n2. The film **Grievous Bodily Harm** is a **1988 Australian** crime film directed by Mark Joffe, starring Colin Friels and John Waters, and it grossed $82,267 at the box office in **Australia**[2][3] — so the country in question is **Australia**.\n3. During the Vietnam War era, **Australia introduced conscription in 1964** through the National Service Act 1964 (under the Menzies government), with conscripted national servicemen subsequently being sent to serve in Vietnam.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 68829.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:94e583be9e34ba8c8cec05546807a1516b652c80be180e9b", "question": "In which year did Taifa of Francisco Giner de los Rios' birthplace cease to exist?", "answers": ["1065"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Francisco Giner de los Ríos was born in **Ronda**, Spain (10 October 1839)[1].\n\nRonda historically was the center of the **Taifa of Ronda**, an independent Muslim kingdom that ceased to exist in **1065**, when it was conquered and annexed by the Taifa of Seville under al-Mu'tamid.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 99332.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:8cad76dd1dfee0d4847e99f55e46c401a1087fde2aee2cc8", "question": "Who did the person considered the founder of three major monotheistic religions marry after the death of Sarah?", "answers": ["Keturah"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Abraham — regarded as the patriarch/founder figure of Judaism, Christianity, and Islam — married **Keturah** after the death of Sarah. According to the Book of Genesis, Keturah was a wife (and concubine) of Abraham, whom he married after his first wife Sarah died; together they had six sons: Zimran, Jokshan, Medan, Midian, Ishbak, and Shuah [1][4][5].\n\nOne additional note from tradition: some rabbinic sources (e.g., an opinion in Genesis Rabbah popularized by Rashi) identify Keturah as being the same person as Hagar, though this view was rejected by other commentators like Ibn Ezra and Nahmanides, and has no explicit basis in the biblical text [2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 86631.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:f4f04befbe7bb6e1ca4055ffd495356951166e116a15b3de", "question": "Who is the spouse of the current queen of England?", "answers": ["Philip Mountbatten"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on my general knowledge, the current queen of England — Queen Elizabeth II — was married to **Prince Philip, Duke of Edinburgh** (they married in 1947 and remained married until his death in April 2021).\n\nPlease note: the knowledge base does contain entries referencing Elizabeth II's marriage [6], but the retrieved snippets did not explicitly state the spouse's name, so I'm answering from general knowledge rather than verified retrieved evidence.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 173280.5, "error": ""}
