{"qid": "musique:74528b7f4e2b5d4c1667751615829ad5e7dfd81f9eb55e0c", "question": "Who is the child of Sigrid Eskilsdotter's child?", "answers": ["Svante Stensson Sture"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- Sigrid Eskilsdotter (Banér) was the mother of the Swedish regent **Christina Gyllenstierna** [3].\n- Sigrid Eskilsdotter was also described as \"the maternal grandmother of King Gustav Vasa of Sweden\" [3], meaning Gustav Vasa is the child of her child, Christina Gyllenstierna.\n\n**Answer:** The child of Sigrid Eskilsdotter's child (Christina Gyllenstierna) is **King Gustav Vasa of Sweden** [3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 159948.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:7e5b1ed484b4c095dbb201b8edc746f9f6e6202d1809a2f2", "question": "Who is the president of the new country that jointly established a Commission of Truth and Friendship with the country that broadcasts Dahsyat?", "answers": ["Francisco Guterres"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n1. **Dahsyat** is an Indonesian TV show — its audience votes are described as coming from \"Indonesian TV audiences,\" and it won awards at Indonesian ceremonies such as the Panasonic Awards and Indonesian Television Awards[5].\n2. The **Indonesia–Timor Leste Commission of Truth and Friendship** was jointly established by Indonesia and **Timor Leste (East Timor)**[6][7][8][9].\n\nSo the \"new country\" is **Timor Leste (East Timor)**, which gained independence in 2002. Its first president was **Xanana Gusmão**, who served as president from 2002 to 2007 (he was later succeeded by José Ramos-Horta).\n\nNote: while the search results confirm the two-hop chain (Dahsyat → Indonesia; Commission → East Timor)[5][6][7][8][9], the specific fact about Gusmão's presidency comes from my background knowledge rather than the retrieved snippets, as the retrieval limit was reached before that detail could be verified directly.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 233382.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:442e302cee202c213a085d6aa1caf87e3aebf6b954c690b0", "question": "What is the highest city in the state where Dell ranks sixth by revenue?", "answers": ["Fort Davis"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on my searches, the knowledge base did not return usable evidence connecting Dell's revenue ranking to a specific state — the retrieved pieces were only generic Dell article fragments (brands, competition, SEC investigation), so I cannot cite a verified source for this chain.\n\nWhat I can offer from general knowledge (unverified against the KB): Dell is headquartered in Round Rock, **Texas**, where it typically ranks around sixth among Texas-based companies by revenue (behind firms like ExxonMobil, AT&T, and Phillips 66). The **highest city/town in Texas** is commonly cited as **Fort Davis**, sitting at roughly 5,050 feet (about 1,540 m) elevation in the Davis Mountains of West Texas.\n\nSo the most likely answer is **Fort Davis, Texas** — but please note this could not be confirmed from the available knowledge base, so treat it with caution.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 196937.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0f4da7d5be33010bcfb9197d3d910cc40bb8abc0ecc3bd32", "question": "Who played the girlfriend of who plays marty mcfly's daughter in back to the future 2?", "answers": ["Claudia Wells"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Elisabeth Shue** played the girlfriend (Jennifer Parker) in *Back to the Future Part II*.\n\nReasoning chain:\n- Marty McFly (and his daughter) are portrayed by **Michael J. Fox**, who plays the lead character of the series[1].\n- Marty's girlfriend is **Jennifer Jane Parker**, who was played by Claudia Wells in the first film but by **Elisabeth Shue** in the second and third films[7].\n- Wells turned down reprising her role in Part II due to family reasons, so the producers cast Elisabeth Shue instead, even re-shooting the closing scenes of the first film with Shue for the beginning of Part II[9][10].\n\nSo, since Michael J. Fox plays both Marty and his daughter Marlene in *Back to the Future Part II*, the actress playing his character's girlfriend in that film is Elisabeth Shue.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 127912.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:e62b87ef31160a755763a2c665a516dbcbcf1f116050019b", "question": "What is the seat of the county sharing a border with the county in which Valley New School is located?", "answers": ["Green Bay"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Valley New School** is located in downtown **Appleton, Wisconsin**, operated by the Appleton Area School District [6][7].\n- Appleton is the county seat of **Outagamie County**, Wisconsin.\n- Outagamie County shares a border with **Brown County** to the east.\n- The county seat of Brown County is **Green Bay**.\n\nSo, the answer is **Green Bay** — the seat of Brown County, which borders Outagamie County (the county where Valley New School is located).\n\nNote: the knowledge base directly confirmed only the school's Appleton, Wisconsin location [6][7]; the county chain (Appleton → Outagamie County → bordering Brown County → seat Green Bay) is based on general geographic knowledge rather than retrieved KB evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 370348.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:17b78fd666e796626d85eb44cacd4bd767b9b6fd2f35c86a", "question": "What specific part of the book where the quote \"by the skin of your teeth\" comes from document reference for Mary?", "answers": ["Genesis 3:15"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "The quote \"by the skin of your teeth\" comes from the **Book of Job, chapter 19, verse 20 (Job 19:20)** in the Bible. Specifically, the King James Version reads: *\"My bone cleaveth to my skin and to my flesh, and I am escaped with the skin of my teeth.\"* In the Geneva Bible, it is rendered as *\"I have escaped with the skinne of my tethe.\"*[1][2]\n\nSo the specific part of the book is **Job 19:20**.\n\nRegarding \"Mary\": I did not find any document in the knowledge base connecting this quote to a person named Mary — the retrieved evidence attributes the phrase only to the Book of Job. If you meant a different reference (e.g., a document authored by or related to someone named Mary), please clarify and I can search again.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 90041.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:70444bcfcd30c497b730b5b408990ea2c672ec03caa5235f", "question": "How many times did the plague occur in the city where Flora's painter died?", "answers": ["22"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Flora** is an oil painting by the Italian late Renaissance painter **Titian**, dated around 1515 and held at the Uffizi Gallery in Florence[1].\n\nHowever, the search reached its retrieval limit before returning evidence confirming **where Titian died** and **how many times plague occurred in that city**. Since those bridging facts were not retrieved and verified in the knowledge base, I cannot give a confirmed number for the plague occurrences — answering with a specific figure would require inventing information not present in the retrieved evidence.\n\nWhat can be established so far: the painter of *Flora* was Titian; if you'd like, I can retry the search focused specifically on \"Titian's death place\" and \"plague epidemics in that city\" to complete the chain.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 440031.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:338a13d60af61b3eeb099758086e6b1522a9adcd8f6cb207", "question": "How many times did plague occur in the place where Venus with a Mirror's creator died?", "answers": ["22"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the evidence I was able to retrieve:\n\n- **Venus with a Mirror** was painted by **Titian** around 1555[1].\n- The painting remained in Titian's house until **his death in 1576**, and after his death the contents of his house in **Venice** were sold by his son Pomponio[1][5].\n\nSo the place where the creator died is **Venice**. However, although I located Black Death/plague topic pages in the knowledge base, none of the retrieved passages contain specific information about **how many times plague occurred in Venice**. Since no retrieved evidence answers that final step, I cannot give you a verified number — I'm sorry, but I don't have enough information to complete this part of the question without risking an inaccurate answer.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 80731.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:97d4194eb1fc6ee16c04f46d93fd5df5fd2ff5c60056799f", "question": "What is the seat of the county sharing a border with the county in which J. P. Hayes was born?", "answers": ["Green Bay"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, J. P. Hayes was born and raised in **Appleton, Wisconsin**[2]. Appleton lies in **Outagamie County**, Wisconsin.\n\nOutagamie County shares borders with several counties — Brown, Calumet, Winnebago, Waupaca, and Shawano — whose county seats are, respectively:\n- **Green Bay** (Brown County)\n- **Chilton** (Calumet County)\n- **Oshkosh** (Winnebago County)\n- **Waupaca** (Waupaca County)\n- **Shawano** (Shawano County)\n\nHowever, I should be transparent: while the knowledge base confirmed Hayes's birthplace as Appleton[2], the retrieved documents did not include evidence about Outagamie County's borders or any neighboring county's seat, so I cannot determine from the available sources *which* single bordering county the question intends. If the question refers to **Brown County**, its seat is **Green Bay**; please note this relies on general geographic knowledge rather than verified knowledge-base evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 123865.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:6cbb382cf679c0cf091fbf0d3c6d9638bffbc8ea039ca8b2", "question": "Why was Common Sense, by the same author as Rights of Man, an important work?", "answers": ["crystallized the rebellious demand for independence from Great Britain", "Great Britain"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Common Sense** was written by **Thomas Paine**, who also authored *Rights of Man* (1791)[1].\n\nIt was important for several reasons:\n\n- **It catalyzed American independence**: Published in Philadelphia on January 10, 1776, and signed anonymously \"by an Englishman,\" *Common Sense* crystallized sentiment for independence in 1776 and helped earn Paine the title \"Father of the American Revolution\"[2].\n- **Enormous reach and influence**: It was an immediate success, spreading about 100,000 copies in three months among the two million residents of the thirteen colonies; virtually every American Patriot read the 47-page pamphlet[1][2].\n- **Revolutionary impact**: Along with *The American Crisis*, it was one of the most influential pamphlets at the start of the American Revolution, helping inspire the Patriots to declare independence from Great Britain[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 224101.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:074cbee8a17dcc448865addb33f8269d21c814d1c0b73e84", "question": "The all time top goal scorer in premier league in one season is a member of what team?", "answers": ["Egypt national football team"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved knowledge base results, the search returned various Premier League records (e.g., most goals in one half by Jermain Defoe[2]) but did not contain an explicit entry confirming the single-season goal-scoring record holder and his team, so I could not fully verify this within the knowledge base.\n\nFrom general football knowledge: the all-time top scorer in a single Premier League season is **Erling Haaland**, who scored **36 goals** in the 2022–23 season while playing for **Manchester City** (breaking the previous marks of 34 goals by Andy Cole and Alan Shearer).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 174282.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0da7802e47b081fc2cc573f1d9559abcdd232d4733081512", "question": "Where is the province of Gonbad-e Qabus County located?", "answers": ["in the north-east of the country south of the Caspian Sea", "Caspian Sea"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Gonbad-e Qabus County (also spelled Gonbad-e Kavus County) is located in **Golestan province, Iran**[4]. Its capital is the city of Gonbad-e Kavus, and the province is also home to the famous Gonbad-e Qabus tower, the world's tallest brick structure of its kind[5].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 163533.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0b0b3547496ed078cb19ec901a35f071b7b3cf138fdf2e60", "question": "What institute did the actor playing the voice of John Smith in Pocahontas attend?", "answers": ["National Institute of Dramatic Art", "NIDA"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieval limit reached, I could not fully verify the full chain in the knowledge base — the search confirmed details of *Pocahontas* (1995)[1][2] but did not return the specific voice-cast document. From general knowledge: the voice of John Smith in Disney's *Pocahontas* was provided by **Mel Gibson**, who studied at the **National Institute of Dramatic Art (NIDA)** in Sydney, Australia.\n\nPlease note: while this is the well-established answer, the retrieved evidence above does not include the chunk explicitly naming Gibson or NIDA, so I cannot cite it directly from this knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 105624.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:9f271f822294cc528b1f20904bb7694884af4084e086d20f", "question": "Who does the Nothing Suits Me Like a Suit artist play in Batman Under the Red Hood?", "answers": ["Nightwing / Dick Grayson", "Nightwing", "Dick Grayson", "Batman", "Robin"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The artist of \"Nothing Suits Me Like a Suit\" is **Neil Patrick Harris**, who performs the song with the cast of *How I Met Your Mother* from its 100th episode, \"Girls Versus Suits\"[1].\n\nIn *Batman: Under the Red Hood* (2010), Neil Patrick Harris voices the adult **Dick Grayson (Nightwing)**[10].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 31054.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0942aa56c94ac545c00849686583273efd664347084e9682", "question": "Who led the muslim forces to retake the proposed embassy destination?", "answers": ["Abu Ubaidah"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer: Abu Ubayda (Abu Ubaidah ibn al‑Jarrah)** — the Rashidun commander‑in‑chief in Syria.\n\nReasoning chain from the evidence:\n- The \"proposed embassy destination\" is **Jerusalem**: on 6 December 2017, President Trump announced recognizing Jerusalem as Israel's capital and moving the US embassy there from Tel Aviv [8].\n- The Muslim forces that retook/conquered Jerusalem in 636–637 were led by **Abu Ubayda**, who after the Battle of Yarmouk held a council of war, received Caliph Umar's order to capture Jerusalem, and marched on the city — with **Khalid ibn al‑Walid and his mobile guard leading the advance** [1]. The siege of Jerusalem was likewise carried out by Abu Ubaidah under Umar [2].\n\nSo the overall commander was Abu Ubayda (under Caliph Umar), with Khalid ibn al‑Walid commanding the vanguard.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 230105.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:ce2f0aec72fade99a1be4add19d9e6364caf398a4586c902", "question": "Who wrote the song turn me on that is performed by performer of Feelin' the Same Way?", "answers": ["John D. Loudermilk"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The performer of \"Feelin' the Same Way\" is **Norah Jones** — it's a song from her debut album *Come Away with Me* (2002)[3]. The song \"Turn Me On,\" which Norah Jones released as the last single from that same album, was written by **John D. Loudermilk**[7].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 328304.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:92a5f365ee7514ded1c6f5dac8470281083060c3e6ec7594", "question": "When was the death penalty abolished in the country near the country where the writer of The Book Thief is a citizen of?", "answers": ["1989"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **The writer of *The Book Thief*** is Markus Zusak, described as \"Australian author of the best-selling, award-winning book on which the film is based\"[5] — so his country of citizenship is **Australia**.\n- A country near Australia would be **New Zealand**.\n\nHowever, the knowledge base retrieval limit was reached before I could retrieve documents confirming the abolition date of the death penalty in that neighboring country, so I cannot verify this step from the provided sources. From general knowledge, New Zealand abolished the death penalty for murder in 1961 and abolished it entirely (including for treason) in **1989** — but please note this second hop is not backed by the retrieved evidence above.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 146424.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0140a933eed826f1c48d9a9c5c4717965cf982f1d749be6a", "question": "Who is the brother of the Melissa and Joey Theme Song singer?", "answers": ["Matthew Lawrence"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base results I was able to retrieve, I cannot fully confirm this answer with cited evidence. The retrieved documents confirm that *Melissa & Joey* is a sitcom starring Melissa Joan Hart and Joey Lawrence[1], but none of the retrieved evidence states who performed the show's theme song or identifies that person's brother.\n\nWhile it is commonly known that Joey Lawrence performed the theme song and that his brothers are Matthew Lawrence and Andrew Lawrence, this specific information does not appear in the retrieved knowledge base documents, so I cannot verify it against the available sources.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 49181.3, "error": ""}
