{"qid": "musique:77351e24c1b3474414472073259cea34aa2b7c63234264a6", "question": "Who sings Home Alone Tonight with the singer of \"I Don't Want This Night to End\"?", "answers": ["Karen Fairchild"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Karen Fairchild** (of Little Big Town) sings \"Home Alone Tonight\" with **Luke Bryan**.\n\nReasoning chain:\n- The singer of \"I Don't Want This Night to End\" is Luke Bryan — the retrieved evidence describes how Bryan explained the song's meaning and co-wrote it with Rhett Akins, Dallas Davidson, and Ben Hayslip (The Peach Pickers)[4].\n- \"Home Alone Tonight\" is Luke Bryan's 2015 duet with Karen Fairchild of Little Big Town, released from his album *Kill the Lights*.\n\nNote: the knowledge base confirmed Luke Bryan's link to \"I Don't Want This Night to End\"[4], but did not return a document on \"Home Alone Tonight,\" so that second step relies on general knowledge rather than retrieved evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 54482.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:28e93095b912403dad071f2821bcf6c6bef250f5c506ccc7", "question": "Who was the first black student admitted to where Robert Khayat was educated?", "answers": ["James Howard Meredith", "James Meredith"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**James Meredith** was the first black student admitted to the **University of Mississippi** — where Robert Khayat was educated.\n\n- Robert Khayat attended Moss Point High School and the University of Mississippi, earning both a Bachelor of Arts and a Juris Doctor degree there (he later also received an LL.M. from Yale University) [1]. He was also the university's 15th Chancellor and played football for Ole Miss [5][1].\n- James Meredith became the first African-American student to enroll at the University of Mississippi on October 1, 1962, after federal and state forces took control following riots; his admission is regarded as a pivotal moment in U.S. civil rights history [6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 41731.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0d59b836b70226e7bbd830db4030b09426a37d8543da426d", "question": "What record label is the performer who released All Your Faded Things on?", "answers": ["Kill Rock Stars"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**Cold Crush Records.** *All Your Faded Things* is an album released by performer Anna Oxygen on July 22, 2003, under the Cold Crush Records label (produced by Justin Trosper)[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 32833.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:a6e0032c62ece331c56364ced53e1c4076c303e4ae287074", "question": "What is the capital of the county that shares a border with the county where WAPL is licensed to broadcast?", "answers": ["Green Bay"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Answer: Green Bay** (the county seat of Brown County, Wisconsin).\n\nReasoning chain:\n1. WAPL (105.7 FM) is a classic rock station **licensed to Appleton, Wisconsin**, serving the Green Bay and Appleton–Oshkosh areas; its transmitter facilities are located in southeastern **Brown County**[1].\n2. Appleton, the community of license, is located in **Outagamie County**, which shares a border with **Brown County** (the adjacent county referenced in WAPL's own facility description)[1].\n3. The county seat (\"capital\") of Brown County is **Green Bay**.\n\nNote: the knowledge base confirmed WAPL's licensing to Appleton[1], but the searches did not return documents detailing the full list of Outagamie County's neighbors or their seats, so the county-border and county-seat steps rely on general geographic knowledge rather than retrieved evidence. If you had a different bordering county in mind (e.g., Winnebago → Oshkosh, Calumet → Chilton, Shawano → Shawano, Waupaca → Waupaca), let me know and I can narrow it down.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 117565.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:170fdcb3bc67d3485e1d9119c94ecb4accbd49d1a5b342ee", "question": "In what year did Margaret Knox's spouse pass away?", "answers": ["1572"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Margaret Knox had two spouses:\n\n- **John Knox** (her first husband, the Scottish reformer) died in **November 1572** [7].\n- Her second husband, **Sir Andrew Ker of Faldonside**, whom she married in January 1574, died on **19 December 1599** [7].\n\nSo depending on which spouse you mean: John Knox passed away in **1572**, and Sir Andrew Ker in **1599**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 42663.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:6448aa2df921d10654846a32887ee601b9456dd05da18428", "question": "What is the record label of the co-writer and recording artist of Permission to Fly?", "answers": ["Hollywood Records"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved context, the document \"Permission to Fly\" exists in the knowledge base, though the returned snippets only showed section headings (Singles, Credits, Composition, Critical reception, Release history) rather than full text.\n\nTo the best of my knowledge: **Permission to Fly** is the 2009 studio album by American pop singer **Jordan Pruitt**, who both co-wrote songs on and recorded the album. Her record label was **Hollywood Records**.\n\nPlease note: since the retrieval results contained only section titles and not the underlying credit text, I could not fully verify the label detail against the source chunks, so treat this answer with mild caution.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 116562.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:45b3ff5fd17fddc64c647566139dadd2693cf4488c0e6ec7", "question": "Who did the original voice of the character played by mila kunis in the cleveland show?", "answers": ["Lacey Chabert"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on what the knowledge base returned, I can confirm the core Cleveland Show cast details: Mike Henry voices Cleveland Brown and Rallo Tubbs[1], Reagan Gomez-Preston plays Roberta Tubbs after Nia Long voiced her for the first thirteen episodes[3], Kevin Michael Richardson plays Cleveland Jr., Jason Sudeikis plays Holt Richter and Terry Kimple[3], and Seth MacFarlane/Jess Harnell voice Tim the Bear[5].\n\nFor your specific question: Mila Kunis's signature animated character is **Meg Griffin** (a role she carried over whenever Meg appeared in *Family Guy*-related content including The Cleveland Show universe). Meg Griffin's **original voice was Lacey Chabert**, who voiced the character during the show's earliest seasons before Mila Kunis took over the role permanently.\n\nNote: the retrieved documents covered the main Cleveland Show voice cast but did not explicitly contain the Kunis/Meg/Chabert detail, so that final point comes from general knowledge rather than a cited source. If you'd like, I can run another targeted search on the Family Guy cast list to verify it against the knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 142653.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:a3ba409a8a3c8bad40a1fa415b981a8227c7d21936a06f17", "question": "The city where Dmitry Borisovich was born is located where in Russia?", "answers": ["Yaroslavl Oblast"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, Dmitry Borisovich (Дмитрий Борисович, 1253–1294), a Russian nobleman who was Prince of Rostov and Prince of Uglich, was born in **Rostov** in September 1253[3].\n\nRostov (often called Rostov the Great / Ростов Великий, distinct from Rostov-on-Don) is located in **Yaroslavl Oblast**, in the western part of central European Russia, on the shore of Lake Nero. Note that while the knowledge base confirms Rostov as his birthplace[3], the specific oblast location comes from general geographic knowledge rather than a directly retrieved source.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 71525.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:e34d03a801bfa0de0ee1f5ecd06dbee378a6410d5555f7fe", "question": "What league includes the operating group of Al Janoub Stadium?", "answers": ["Qatar Stars League"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Al Janoub Stadium** (in Al Wakrah, Qatar) is used as the home venue of **Al‑Wakrah Sports Club**, taking over from the older Saoud bin Abdulrahman Stadium after the 2022 FIFA World Cup[1].\n\nSo the \"operating group\" here is **Al‑Wakrah Sports Club**, which competes in the **Qatar Stars League** (Qatar's top-flight football league). Note: the knowledge base documents confirm the stadium–club link[1], but the specific league membership of Al‑Wakrah SC was not directly confirmed within the retrieved evidence, so the league identification relies on general football knowledge rather than a cited document.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 87115.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:1c5e70656ffbd6ba33bd853ce406b5efd495e16a3610ed9f", "question": "When was the kingdom that Heptarchy was part of abolished?", "answers": ["918"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Heptarchy** was the era in which Anglo-Saxon kingdoms were founded in sub-Roman Britain, and **Mercia** was one of those kingdoms (one of the three notable Anglic kingdoms), centred on the River Trent in the Midlands of England[1].\n- During its \"Golden Age\" (roughly 600–900), Mercia dominated the other Heptarchy kingdoms south of the Humber[3].\n\nSo the kingdom in question is **Mercia**. However, the retrieved evidence does not contain an explicit statement about the exact date Mercia was abolished. Based on general historical knowledge (not confirmed by the retrieved documents), Mercia ceased to exist as an independent kingdom around **918 AD**, when it was absorbed into the Kingdom of England under Edward the Elder — but please note this specific year could not be verified within the available knowledge base results.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 86248.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:7e74ad75b51dd675970fecb014631c801252981eb2a657d1", "question": "When did the majority party in the body which determines rules of the US House and US Senate gain control of the House?", "answers": ["the 2010 election"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- The body in question is the U.S. Congress (which sets the rules governing both the House and Senate), and its majority party was the **Republican Party** (e.g., holding majorities in both chambers during the 115th Congress)[3][4].\n- That party regained control of the House in the **November 2010 midterm elections**, when Republicans picked up a net 63 seats — erasing Democratic gains from 2006 and 2008 — and took majority control at the start of the next Congress in January 2011[1].\n\n(For context, Republicans had earlier retaken the House in the 1994 midterms before losing it back to Democrats in 2006[4].)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 201863.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:665c0eff8918d3eeecbdd3439e19c078253bde37c2977f2c", "question": "What is the average salary of a working person with the same nationality as the producer of The Wild Women of Chastity Gulch?", "answers": ["$59,039"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**Step 1 – Producer's nationality:** *The Wild Women of Chastity Gulch* is a 1982 American made-for-television Western romantic comedy that was **executive produced by Aaron Spelling**, premiering on ABC on October 31, 1982 [3]. Aaron Spelling is an American, so the relevant nationality is **American**.\n\n**Step 2 – Average salary:** The knowledge base did not return specific salary data within the available retrieval rounds, so based on general knowledge, the average annual salary of a working person in the United States is approximately **$55,000–$60,000 per year** (median weekly/full-time earnings data put typical annual earnings for full-time American workers in this range).\n\nSo, a working person with the same nationality as the producer (American) earns on average roughly **$55,000–$60,000 per year**. Please note this salary figure comes from general knowledge rather than the knowledge base, as no salary-specific documents were retrievable.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 83078.3, "error": ""}
