{"qid": "2wikimqa:3723ec96e3b1af88c73edaa0a5e87f20194d9d212a67b615", "question": "Which magazine was established first, Rock Street Journal or De As?", "answers": ["De As"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**De AS** was established first.\n\n- **De AS**: The first issue of this Dutch anarchist quarterly appeared in **December 1972**[6].\n- **Rock Street Journal**: This monthly magazine covering the rock scene in India and South Asia was started in **January 1993** in Allahabad by Amit Saigal and Shena Gamat Saigal[1].\n\nSo De AS predates Rock Street Journal by about 20 years.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 57698.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:60735709da9cbdd65bab0a11d1a0991ec842fe74d1ab583b", "question": "Which film has the director who died first, The Piper'S Price or The Blonde From Singapore?", "answers": ["The Piper'S Price"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **The Piper's Price** (1917) was directed by **Joe De Grasse**[2]. De Grasse died on May 25, 1940.\n- **The Blonde from Singapore** (1941) was directed by **Edward Dmytryk**, who died in 1999.\n\nTherefore, **The Piper's Price** is the film whose director died first — Joe De Grasse (died 1940) passed away nearly six decades before Edward Dmytryk (died 1999).\n\n(Note: The directorial credit for *The Piper's Price* is confirmed by the knowledge base[2]; the death-date comparison relies on general biographical knowledge, as the retrieval limit prevented further verification of *The Blonde from Singapore*'s details.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 69796.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "2wikimqa:76a7a3db55c966560ad3e8d34037602d4d12714980898b78", "question": "Are H. H. Hunnewell and Bob Flanigan (Singer) of the same nationality?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes. Both were American:\n\n- **H. H. Hunnewell** (Horatio Hollis Hunnewell, 1810–1902) was an American railroad financier, philanthropist, and prominent horticulturist.[1]\n- **Bob Flanigan** (Robert Lee Flanigan, 1926–2011) was an American tenor vocalist and a founding member of the jazz vocal group The Four Freshmen.[7]\n\nSo they shared the same nationality: American.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 36090.7, "error": ""}
