{"qid": "hotpotqa:ef245cc27251f559f3cd900c11c7bf9f303eec3c4b346802", "question": "Where does the city situated where Kellyville Ridge is located?", "answers": ["on the Cumberland Plain"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Kellyville Ridge is a suburb of **Sydney**, in the state of New South Wales, Australia. It is located 41 kilometres north-west of the Sydney central business district, within the local government area of the City of Blacktown, and is part of Greater Western Sydney[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 25734.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:72a769d6d0fd1c5c90974e60ab97e88e6853b9115a7fb85e", "question": "Which utility holding company did Alfred A. Marcus work as a consultant?", "answers": ["Xcel Energy Inc."], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Alfred A. Marcus worked as a consultant for **Xcel Energy**, which is a utility holding company. According to his profile, he has consulted with companies such as 3M, Corning Inc., Xcel Energy, Medtronic, General Mills, and IBM [1].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 43974.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:139d2fcf8763003414ebd0187eb30b94930de13c4ab754a0", "question": "What are some other areas that share a common deer species with the forests of Mara and Mondrem?", "answers": ["New Zealand, Canada, the United States, the Falkland Islands, and Peru."], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\nThe forests of Mara and Mondrem (medieval hunting forests in Cheshire, England) contained **red deer, fallow deer, and roe deer** among their game animals[3]. Other areas that share these deer species include:\n\n- **Great Britain** – which has fallow deer populations documented among its deer species[7]\n- **Belgium** – where roe deer are found[11]\n- **Iran** – where roe deer also occur[12]\n- **Britain** more broadly, as part of the roe deer's distribution range[10][14]\n\nSo, areas such as Great Britain/Britain (sharing fallow deer and roe deer), Belgium, and Iran all share deer species with the forests of Mara and Mondrem.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 98622.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:cae319fad2385d30d79efac0fb61e58518fbdc0703be3b0f", "question": "What actor was also a president that Richard Darman worked with when they were in office?", "answers": ["George H. W. Bush"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Ronald Reagan.**\n\nRichard Darman served in senior government positions during the presidencies of both Ronald Reagan and George H. W. Bush[1]. Of those two presidents, Ronald Reagan was also an actor before entering politics, making him the actor-president Darman worked with while in office.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 90299.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:eac05e5d0936f802e28b0c5081a2c2a1a94fe4f2c20cb2df", "question": "Which person is known for playing in punk bands, Keith Morris or Rome Ramirez?", "answers": ["Keith Morris"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Keith Morris** is the one known for playing in punk bands. He is an American singer and songwriter known as the frontman of the hardcore punk bands Black Flag, Circle Jerks, and Off! — he formed Black Flag at age 21 with guitarist Greg Ginn, later formed the Circle Jerks after leaving Black Flag, and then created the supergroup Off! in 2009 [3].\n\nBy contrast, Rome Ramirez is an American singer and guitarist best known for playing with Eric Wilson from Sublime in the band Sublime with Rome [6], which is not framed in the sources as a punk band career.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 47400.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:afa4eccc7b711ace3270c9cbcd784c971a4f0f8d22629ebf", "question": "Sand Lake Baptist Church is located in a hamlet with a population of what at the 2010 census?", "answers": ["1,693"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Sand Lake Baptist Church is located in **Averill Park**, a hamlet (census-designated place) in Rensselaer County, New York — specifically at 2960 State Route 43[1][2]. Averill Park had a population of **1,693** at the 2010 census.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 63070.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:93d2774c99edbde88789a9f989642ff160871ca09e66e46a", "question": "What is the nationality of the author of Fifty Shades Freed?", "answers": ["English"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The novel *Fifty Shades Freed* was written by **E. L. James**[4], and she is **British** (an English author).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 51737.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:670498f9d7fd2582c5791cad2fe212f580d316fa49a8a984", "question": "Who gave the mother of Alberic II of Spoleto the title \"patricia\" of Rome?", "answers": ["Pope John X"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Pope John X gave her the title. The evidence confirms that Alberic II of Spoleto (912–954) was the son of Marozia by her first husband, Duke Alberic I of Spoleto[2], and that Marozia — a Roman noblewoman and alleged mistress of Pope Sergius III — \"was given the unprecedented titles senatrix ('senatoress') and patricia of Rome by Pope John X\"[3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 76672.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:d9f5437fa85841cb12798def39a30721d3d2d140b4982854", "question": "How are elephants connected to Gajabrishta?", "answers": ["Sanskrit"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Elephants are connected to **Gajabrishta** through both its etymology and its architectural form:\n\n- **Etymology:** In Sanskrit, \"Gaja\" means elephant, while \"Brishta\" refers to the back or hip portion of a sitting elephant [1].\n- **Architecture:** Gajabrishta is therefore a style of temple *vimana* (tower) that resembles the back of a seated elephant. This style was used by the Later Cholas of the Chola empire in the Indian subcontinent, particularly those in Thondai Mandalam around North Tamil Nadu, when constructing their temples [1].\n\nIn short, Gajabrishta is literally named after elephants because these temple towers were designed to evoke the rounded back/hip shape of a sitting elephant.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 65818.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:75439ed8609c12817aaee0fa4f749aebb3e4a36dfa72390e", "question": "Which California band, whose debut album Adrenaline appeared in 1995, has been referred to as \"the Radiohead of metal\"?", "answers": ["Deftones"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Deftones** is the band. Formed in Sacramento, California, in 1988, the American alternative metal band released its debut album *Adrenaline* on October 3, 1995 (via Maverick Records). Their experimental nature has led some critics to describe them as \"the Radiohead of metal.\"", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 47683.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:6320310adc4a826b1bb240c63ac1735b7b29e93a59782364", "question": "What was the Roud Folk Song Index of the nursery rhyme inspiring What Are Little Girls Made Of?", "answers": ["821"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, I can confirm that *What Are Little Girls Made Of?* draws on the nursery rhyme **\"What Are Little Boys Made Of?\"**, which appears in the knowledge base alongside its lyrics section[4]. \n\nHowever, the search results only returned section headings (e.g., \"Lyrics,\" \"Reception,\" \"Plot\") without the full text content, so the specific **Roud Folk Song Index number** for that nursery rhyme was not included in what I was able to retrieve. I therefore cannot verify the exact Roud number from the available evidence and would rather not guess at it.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 49884.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:a16ac581bde4459861c8681858b07f82363f66142beed4c2", "question": "1920 Politics was a Republican political strategy to reassert the authority of the white race in which US state?", "answers": ["Hawaii"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "According to the knowledge base, \"1920 Politics\" was a political strategy to reassert the authority of the white race in **Hawaii** — specifically, what was then the U.S. Territory of Hawaii[4]. \n\nTwo corrections to the question's premise based on the evidence:\n- It was described as a **Democratic** political strategy, not Republican[4].\n- Hawaii was a U.S. **territory** at the time, not yet a state.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 85492.3, "error": ""}
