{"qid": "hotpotqa:3df75e9b85781f83af8d3011cedb0c5fc6725f3489e48e54", "question": "What occupation is shared by both Marge Piercy and Richard Aldington?", "answers": ["poet"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Marge Piercy and Richard Aldington were both **writers** — specifically, both were poets as well as authors of fiction.\n\n- Marge Piercy is described as \"an American progressive activist, feminist, and **writer**,\" known for works like *Woman on the Edge of Time* and *He, She and It* [4].\n- Richard Aldington was \"an English **writer and poet**\" associated with the Imagist movement, whose 50-year career covered poetry, novels, criticism and biography [7].\n\nSo the occupation they share is writer (both poetry and prose/novel writing).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 46313.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:561c378ca284bab34f735f05e21dae8653ca251f6480d6e4", "question": "What time did the show, in which Gordon Burns was the host, usually air on Monday?", "answers": ["7pm"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the show hosted by Gordon Burns was **The Krypton Factor** — he presented it throughout its original 18-year run from 1977 to 1995[1].\n\nHowever, the evidence I was able to retrieve does not specify the programme's usual Monday broadcast time, so I cannot confirm the exact air time from the knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 64464.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:56dbceaf2e826fbb177035f03d7a263d62365856f21f39da", "question": "The Red Queen's Race is a science fiction short story that uses the Red Queen's race from a novel by Lewis Carroll that is the sequel to what?", "answers": ["Alice's Adventures in Wonderland"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Alice's Adventures in Wonderland** (1865).\n\nThe chain: \"The Red Queen's Race\" is a science fiction short story by Isaac Asimov that uses the Red Queen's race from Lewis Carroll's *Through the Looking-Glass* as a metaphor for its final plot twist[1]. *Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There* (1871) is in turn the sequel to Lewis Carroll's *Alice's Adventures in Wonderland*[3].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 47221.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:4636487a1855382398fe1e54284cb7dd3d7b8d178ac4a476", "question": "Migene González-Wippler is leading expert on a religion that developed among which ethnic group ?", "answers": ["West African descendants"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Migene González-Wippler is a Puerto Rican new-age author and a leading expert on the Afro‑Caribbean religion of **Santería**[1]. That religion developed among the **Yoruba** people (of West Africa, carried by enslaved Yoruba to the Caribbean, where Santería emerged among their descendants).", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 59810.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:ee07a420f322205e992788e25c0b256cadcd99abd359ff0f", "question": "What was the home city of the author of the famous novel tracing the life of a male roe deer from his birth onward?", "answers": ["Vienna"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "The novel described is **Bambi, a Life in the Woods** (1923), which traces the life of Bambi, a male roe deer, from his birth onward[1]. Its author was **Felix Salten**, an Austrian writer[1].\n\nSalten's home city was **Vienna** — he moved there as a young child from his birthplace of Budapest and lived there for most of his life. (Note: the retrieved evidence confirms the novel and its author[1], but did not directly return a passage stating his home city; Vienna is given here based on standard biographical knowledge of Felix Salten.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 57230.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:9fe92e20febe504670266d638939a94fc57c3cf8fe9cf948", "question": "The owner of radio station KWPW has the same name as an American character actor. What is it?", "answers": ["Bill McCutcheon"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Bill McCutcheon**。根据检索到的资料，KWPW（107.9 FM，\"Power 108\"）是一家位于德克萨斯州Robinson、服务Waco地区的当代热门音乐电台，其所有者为Bill McCutcheon[1]，这与美国性格演员Bill McCutcheon同名。", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 90699.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:1fd7df3ba224e985f87e976c7a4a67ddc1012a249a542bea", "question": "Hardley Flood is an area of lagoons that support populations of the waterfowl that are a medium sized version of what animal?", "answers": ["duck"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**A duck.**\n\nThe knowledge base links Hardley Flood with the **common pochard**, which is the waterfowl whose populations these lagoons support [1][3]. The common pochard is described as a *medium-sized diving duck* [1][5] — so the medium-sized waterfowl in question is a medium-sized version of a **duck**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 61444.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:a18c0ece7c2ae88d2f9f3ffc5eaa13297a1b5d0125a172ad", "question": "Carlos Salazar Lomelín is the CEO of which Mexican multinational beverage retailer?", "answers": ["Fomento Económico Mexicano"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Carlos Salazar Lomelín is the CEO of **Coca-Cola FEMSA**, a position he has held since 1 January 2000[1][2]. Coca-Cola FEMSA is part of FEMSA (Fomento Económico Mexicano), a Mexican multinational beverage and retail company headquartered in Monterrey, Mexico, which operates the largest independent Coca-Cola bottling group in the world[5].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 57851.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:7528b0be35e8e6007f07c98b9f2d45ee1c854298134aba5c", "question": "Sarah Price and Rob Schneider are both what profession? ", "answers": ["director"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the knowledge base search:\n\n- **Rob Schneider**: The retrieved evidence confirms he is an actor/comedian and filmmaker — he starred as the title character in the CBS sitcom *Rob*, and in 2015 he \"produced, directed and starred in *Real Rob*\" [5]. He also starred in the comedy film *Big Stan* (per topic navigation).\n\n- **Sarah Price**: Unfortunately, the knowledge base search did not return any passages about Sarah Price, so I cannot verify her profession from the retrieved evidence.\n\nThat said, drawing on general knowledge: Sarah Price is an American **film director** (known for documentaries like *American Movie*), and Rob Schneider has also worked as a **film director** (*Big Stan*, *Real Rob*). So the profession they share is most likely **film director** — though I'd note that only Rob Schneider's credentials could be confirmed against the knowledge base here.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 101246.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c16a3dd0b5b97180359715cab5c41d528cf2a99e2744f037", "question": "Are Yut and Tsuro both board games?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes, both are board games:\n\n- **Yut** (also known as Yut nori or yunnori) is a traditional Korean board game, especially played during Korean New Year.[2]\n- **Tsuro** is a board game for two to eight players in which players place tiles and move their pieces along paths, aiming to be the last piece remaining on the board while avoiding paths that end at the board edge.[8]", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 51708.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:83df22880fee9f761464404ca2ff6ab21954e6b0827b4142", "question": "What past honoree from the New Dramatists Organization is a powerful baritone who won the Tony Award for Best Actor in 2000?", "answers": ["Brian Stokes Mitchell"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Brian Stokes Mitchell** is the past honoree matching that description. He is an American actor and singer described as \"a powerful baritone\" and one of Broadway's central leading men since the 1990s, and he won the Tony Award for Best Actor in a Musical in 2000 for his performance as Fred Graham/Petruchio in *Kiss Me, Kate*[1][2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 73152.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:efc793ac73a0341a7cc35ea6872a89be3d4fed5f64c1e043", "question": "The \"New York Times\" bestselling book \"The 50th Law\" contains lessons and anecdotes from this historical figure who was a classical Greek (Athenian) philosopher who is known mainly through the writings of which philosopher?", "answers": ["Plato"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **The historical figure featured in *The 50th Law*** is **Socrates** — the book, co-written by rapper 50 Cent and author Robert Greene, draws on lessons and anecdotes from historical figures including Abraham Lincoln, Sun Tzu, **Socrates**, Napoleon, Malcolm X, and James Baldwin[10].\n\n- **Socrates is known mainly through the writings of his student, Plato** (whose dialogues feature Socrates as their central figure), along with some accounts by Xenophon and Aristophanes.\n\nSo the answer chain is: *The 50th Law* → **Socrates** → known mainly through the writings of **Plato**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 75191.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:826474c90684b1813df9324f779959a5e060d1cfc893a85f", "question": "Santa Bring My Baby Back was released on which fourth Elvis studio album in 1957?", "answers": ["Elvis' Christmas Album"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "\"Santa Bring My Baby Back (To Me)\" — a 1957 Christmas song written by Aaron Schroeder and Claude Demetrius and recorded by Elvis Presley — was released on **Elvis' Christmas Album**, issued by RCA Victor in 1957[2]. This album was Presley's fourth studio album, and the song also appeared on its later reissues, including the 1958 reissue, the 1970 RCA Camden reissue, the 1975 Pickwick reissue, and the 1985 RCA Special Products rerelease[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 84803.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:8fddd32ef1d88a5f8fde49152fc015eb80b3d60d1108d073", "question": "What side-scrolling platform game had Christian Whitehead as project lead?", "answers": ["Sonic Mania"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Sonic Mania** is the side-scrolling platform game led by Christian Whitehead. Development began in 2015, led by Australian programmer Christian \"Taxman\" Whitehead, who along with Headcannon and PagodaWest Games developed and released this original title in the Sonic series in 2017[2][3].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 53035.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:480b33cc38878ea4fe81b796d09ba2d10cca554611e323ec", "question": "What city in the Philippines is home to the Robinsons Galleria and the 4th largest shopping mall in the world?", "answers": ["Metro Manila"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**Quezon City.** Robinsons Galleria (also known as Robinsons Galleria Ortigas) is located at EDSA corner Ortigas Avenue in Quezon City, right beside SM Megamall — the mall often cited as the 4th largest shopping mall in the world[1][2].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 204872.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:bf8ee342564ae21a17b169023f83a20220a63198827a0232", "question": "The Panel with striding lion  was one of many that lined the way north of the gate to the inner city of what location?", "answers": ["Babylon"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Babylon.** According to the knowledge base, the Panel with striding lion (MA 31.13.1), a Neo-Babylonian glazed brick panel dated 604–562 B.C., was one of many that lined the Processional Way north of the Ishtar Gate[4] — the gate leading to the inner city of Babylon.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 60246.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:d6fe5bc54941b566b3c88598bbc459ee4d7098d6c6f22c4d", "question": "What else was Baghdad known as during the Abbasid Caliphate?", "answers": ["the City of Peace"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on my searches of the knowledge base, the retrieved documents (which cover topics like the Abna' al-dawla, Bajkam, and the Buyid takeover of Baghdad) do not contain any passages about alternative names for Baghdad during the Abbasid Caliphate, so I cannot cite supporting evidence for this from the available sources.\n\nFrom general historical knowledge: during the Abbasid Caliphate, Baghdad was officially known as **Madinat al-Salam** (\"City of Peace\"), the name given by its founder, Caliph al-Mansur, when he established it in 762 CE. It was also sometimes referred to as the \"Round City\" (al-Mudawwara) because of its original circular design. However, please note this answer could not be verified against the provided knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 143297.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c1cfe7334235bf4a9ef11293d8be2d4055a70ac89334be2c", "question": "What title did the author of Det norske Theater's first production hold ? ", "answers": ["Baron of Holberg"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Det norske Theater's opening production was **Den Vægelsindede**, a comedy by **Ludvig Holberg**, staged at the theatre's premiere on 2 January 1850[1]. Holberg, the author of that play, held the title of **Professor at the University of Copenhagen** — he was a renowned scholar and playwright whose work formed the foundation of Danish-Norwegian literature and theater.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 102662.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c6c1401ec6e635eac28b985621cf59bde9e416cac2b30080", "question": "Are Dutch Smoushond and Sussex Spaniel's both dog breeds?", "answers": ["yes"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Yes, both are dog breeds:\n\n- **Dutch Smoushond** – \"a small breed of dog\" related to the Pinscher and Schnauzer type, originating in the Netherlands/Germany[2].\n- **Sussex Spaniel** – \"a breed of dog native to Sussex in southern England\"[10].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 65377.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:861e7d3ba836f6a816d0265dc1c9f5566ef4d22b8de223ca", "question": "John Paesano wrote which film for DreamWorks Dragons?", "answers": ["Dragons: Riders of Berk"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the evidence retrieved, I cannot confirm that John Paesano wrote any film for *DreamWorks Dragons*.\n\nWhat the knowledge base does show:\n- **John Paesano** is an American composer (born July 2, 1977) known for collaborating with director Wes Ball on *The Maze Runner* film series (*The Maze Runner*, *The Scorch Trials*, *The Death Cure*), composing for the Marvel TV series *Daredevil* and *The Defenders*, and scoring video games such as *Detroit: Become Human* and *Marvel's Spider-Man* (winning a BAFTA for *Miles Morales*)[7][8].\n- The *DreamWorks Dragons* articles retrieved cover plot, accolades, home media, and character details (e.g., Stoick the Vast)[1][2][3][4], but none of them credit John Paesano with a film.\n\nSo, while Paesano is clearly documented as a film/game composer in the sources, there is no retrieved evidence linking him to a *DreamWorks Dragons* film — the connection appears to be a misconception, and I'd rather say I don't have evidence for it than guess.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 139523.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:0d6a7aae6f7e00180107c47a73dedda18caf3949666c4d55", "question": "Which American actor portray Steve Urkel in \"Family Matters\"", "answers": ["Jaleel Ahmad White"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Steve Urkel is portrayed by **Jaleel White**, an American actor, in the ABC/CBS sitcom *Family Matters*. The character was originally intended as a one-time guest appearance but became an instant hit, making White a regular cast member for the show's entire nine-season run (1989–1998)[1][2].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 50601.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c5f1f53de09eb82eae93ae68a9581141a8cc8f3df6851926", "question": "What lake in Australia is named after a Scottish Knight?", "answers": ["Lake Wallace"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Lake Wallace** (in New South Wales, Australia) is named after Sir William Wallace, the famous Scottish knight[4]. The knowledge base contains entries for both Lake Wallace[4] and William Wallace, the medieval Scottish knight who led the Wars of Scottish Independence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 90318.3, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:40b5d54934bcf22bb9267e605c49d46509f3bd41b1a34c31", "question": "what is the group called that Dianne Morgan and Joe Wilkinson a part of in the BBC comedy \"Two Episodes of Mash\"", "answers": ["the deadpan sketch group"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Diane Morgan and Joe Wilkinson formed a sketch comedy duo called **Two Episodes of Mash**[1]. From 2008, they performed at the Edinburgh Festival Fringe for three consecutive years, and in 2012 the act completed its second BBC radio series (co-starring David O'Doherty) and appeared in BBC Three's Live at the Electric[1].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 66478.8, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:c9ee71b5dc7f5a4538d7727c95f80c174d3c9819dfbeac5a", "question": "The 53rd National Hockey League All-Star Game took place at the indoor arena that was completed in what year?", "answers": ["1998"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the available retrieval results, I was unable to retrieve a document directly confirming the venue of the 53rd NHL All-Star Game (the search returned documents on the 6th, 52nd, and 63rd games instead), so I cannot cite knowledge-base evidence for this specific chain.\n\nFrom general knowledge, however: the 53rd National Hockey League All-Star Game was held on February 3, 2001, at the **Pepsi Center** in Denver, Colorado — an indoor arena that was **completed/opened in 1999**.\n\nPlease note this answer comes from background knowledge rather than verified knowledge-base evidence, as the targeted documents were not surfaced before the retrieval limit was reached.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 78548.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:1df269c3eca341348a837f39c628f59aa40d663c07875d24", "question": "For what ogranization does a commentator of Press TV serve as associate director?", "answers": ["Henry Jackson Society"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the information available, the Press TV commentator in question is **Catherine Shakdam**, a political analyst who served as a commentator for Press TV, and she served as **associate director of the Shafaqna Institute for Middle Eastern Studies**.\n\nNote: The retrieved knowledge base chunks confirmed background details about Press TV (an Iranian state-owned English-language news network under IRIB)[1][2], but did not directly contain the sentence naming the commentator's role, so I am answering based on best available knowledge rather than fully verified retrieved evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 353844.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:220004b7b3836d023f1e416666c0d8d43ae75a77c1a8bdd0", "question": "Who was the first African American student at the university Robert Khayat was educated at?", "answers": ["James Howard Meredith", "James Meredith"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\nRobert Khayat was educated at the **University of Mississippi** (Ole Miss), where he received both his Bachelor of Arts and Juris Doctor degrees, and played football for the Ole Miss Rebels from 1957–1959[1].\n\nAs for the second part of your question — the first African American student at the University of Mississippi is widely known to be **James Meredith**, who enrolled in 1962 amid the famous Ole Miss riot. Notably, James Meredith appears alongside Robert Khayat in the same knowledge base topic cluster. However, I was unable to retrieve a source document directly verifying this detail before reaching the search limit, so while this is a well-established historical fact, I cannot point you to a specific citation for it from this knowledge base.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 77404.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:30dac2419bdedc98a7dac06e6d4c8046262c2d0197c2b7f9", "question": "Mehmet Hayri Tarhan's birthplace is the capital of what municipality?", "answers": ["Malko Tarnovo Municipality"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Mehmet Hayri Tarhan was born in Tirnovadjik — Malko Tarnovo [1]. That town is the main town of **Malko Tarnovo Municipality**, a municipality in Burgas Province, Bulgaria, which includes the town of Malko Tarnovo and a number of surrounding villages [9].\n\nSo his birthplace is the capital of **Malko Tarnovo Municipality**.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 71085.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:096c9e28af3c91a52463c424203181b2a9c114c3b782dbf5", "question": "Where is Sean Hampton's birth place in the state of Florida?", "answers": ["in Northern Florida", "Northern Florida"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Sean Hampton was born in **Ocala, Florida**[2]. He is an American actor, director, and producer, born on February 1, 1981[1], and is the youngest of five children of a dentist father and a professional model mother[2].", "tool_calls": ["knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 33467.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "hotpotqa:f2da37b927d3f8ce752b69d7149a6eb31aa738d123f8fdb6", "question": "Which artist is known for his work on Marvel Team-Up and Batman: Son of the Demon?", "answers": ["Gerald \"Jerry\" Joseph Bingham, Jr."], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Jerry Bingham** is the artist known for both works. He is the artist of *Batman: Son of the Demon*, the 1987 DC Comics graphic novel written by Mike W. Barr[3], and he also contributed pencil art to *Marvel Team-Up* during his comics career.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 135376.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:78b24e393c76b4fcd88852e310548721c5eea12bb8848aca", "question": "Who was the British general in the battle named after the birth city of the person who recorded Mother-in-Law?", "answers": ["General Edward Pakenham", "Edward Pakenham"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n1. **\"Mother-in-Law\" was recorded by Ernie K-Doe** — a 1961 number-one hit on both the Billboard Hot 100 and R&B charts[4].\n2. **Ernie K-Doe's city is New Orleans** — the Mother-in-Law Lounge dedicated to his memory is located in New Orleans, Louisiana, where he was a central figure in local music[5][6].\n3. **The battle named after his birth city** is therefore the **Battle of New Orleans** (fought in January 1815 during the War of 1812).\n\nThe British general at the Battle of New Orleans was **Major General Sir Edward Pakenham**, who led the British forces and was killed in the battle against Andrew Jackson's American troops.\n\n(Note: the knowledge base directly confirmed steps 1–2; the identification of Pakenham relies on general historical knowledge rather than retrieved documents.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 117984.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:2df2586b40a9e5928817855c3b3a44c45b0809376372f38d", "question": "Who is the president of the new country who has jointly established a Commission on Truth and Friendship with the birth country of Satyo Husodo?", "answers": ["Francisco Guterres"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "**Xanana Gusmão** — the first president of East Timor (Timor-Leste).\n\nReasoning chain:\n- Satyo Husodo was born in **Indonesia** (he is an Indonesian former footballer)[1].\n- Indonesia jointly established the **Indonesia–Timor Leste Commission of Truth and Friendship** with East Timor[6].\n- East Timor is the \"new country\": it became independent on 20 May 2002 when its constitution came into force and it was recognized by the UN[11][14].\n- Upon independence, **Xanana Gusmão** was elected as the country's first president[14].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 92724.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:a9233be5f19b456544331e69848af8bbf56256ce9ed0da78", "question": "In what region of the country of S-Fone is The place of birth of John Phan located?", "answers": ["South Central Coast"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "S-Fone's country is Vietnam — it was a CDMA mobile communication operator founded in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam[8]. John Phan was born in Da Nang, Vietnam[1], so his birthplace lies within the same country where S-Fone operated. Within that country, Da Nang is located in the central part — the Central Vietnam (South Central Coast) region.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 89122.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:de9c2fbc33bc6136a3a0598a7a1ad678882b4a8613f3900b", "question": "The Live Life Loud album's band signed to which label?", "answers": ["Fair Trade Services"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, *Live Life Loud* is the fourth studio album by the Christian rock band **Hawk Nelson**, released on September 22, 2009[3].\n\nHowever, the retrieved evidence confirms the band's identity but does not contain any information about the record label Hawk Nelson signed to, so I cannot verify the label name from the knowledge base sources. While Hawk Nelson is generally known to have been associated with Tooth & Nail Records / its imprint BEC Recordings, that detail did not appear in the retrieved documents, so I cannot confirm it here.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 98516.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:73b7efc9538e65dda454b5be9d9b84a35decf082cd4091be", "question": "What is the source of the river Orlam clans crossed to migrate to Namibia?", "answers": ["Thaba Putsoa"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "The river the Orlam clans crossed when migrating into Namibia is the **Orange River**, and its source is in the **Drakensberg mountains in Lesotho**[1]. From there it flows westwards through South Africa to the Atlantic Ocean, and its basin extends from Lesotho into South Africa and Namibia[1].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 78806.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:5c835b4c358e5f2e8c577dbc38c4857599539968e593a322", "question": "What is the record label of the Metalworks Studio founder?", "answers": ["TML Entertainment"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**TML Entertainment** — the band's own record label.\n\nThe reasoning chain:\n1. Metalworks Studio (via Metalworks Institute) was founded by **Gil Moore**, drummer of the Canadian rock band Triumph[3].\n2. Triumph launched its own label after its 10-year/5-album contract with MCA Records expired at the end of 1994. It was initially known as TRC (1995–2003), then renamed **TML Entertainment** in 2003 with the release of *Live at the US Festival*. The label is distributed by Universal Music Group in Canada and ADA (Warner Music Group subsidiary) in the US[6].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 115346.2, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:f6ab8327fd10a883614cd3f571224c82f1cb7073de8a1143", "question": "What movie did the person who acted in the comedy film Crime School win his only Oscar for?", "answers": ["The African Queen"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the comedy film **Crime School** starred **Humphrey Bogart** — the Dead End Kids received top billing over him in the film's advertising[4].\n\nHumphrey Bogart won his only Academy Award (Best Actor) for **The African Queen** (1951).\n\nNote: the retrieved evidence confirms Bogart's role in Crime School[4], while his Oscar-winning film is based on general knowledge, as the knowledge base did not return a chunk specifically documenting his Academy Award.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 83849.4, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:61193402df0b1548ecd9c722ee89b4354d336ded37b4cf49", "question": "with what movie did Mary Philips' husband win his only oscar?", "answers": ["The African Queen"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Mary Philips' husband was actor **Humphrey Bogart**[6]. Bogart won his only Academy Award (Best Actor) for **The African Queen** (1951)[7].", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 93188.0, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:16db4ccc32ee8b04e3daee094ef5e8cfb985a4f0c2c999d5", "question": "Which is the body of water near George Mills' place of birth?", "answers": ["River Thames"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "George Mills (the footballer) was born in **Deptford** on 29 December 1908[1]. Deptford is a district on the south bank of the **River Thames** in London, so the body of water near his place of birth is the River Thames.\n\n(Note: the knowledge base confirmed his birthplace as Deptford[1], but did not contain an explicit statement about the adjacent body of water; the Thames association is based on general geographic knowledge of Deptford.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "entity_relation_lookup"], "latency_ms": 84535.9, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:e935f6dea75d4f17c5f98b7e255950372a22ab83f9632876", "question": "Who started the foundation that developed the Free Software Directory?", "answers": ["Richard Stallman", "rms"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the Free Software Directory (FSD) is a project of the **Free Software Foundation (FSF)**[4], which catalogs free software running under free operating systems such as GNU and Linux, and was formerly co-run by UNESCO[4].\n\nAs for who started that foundation: the Free Software Foundation was founded by **Richard Stallman** in 1985. Note that while the link between the Free Software Directory and the FSF is directly confirmed in the knowledge base [4], the retrieved results did not contain an explicit statement about Stallman founding the FSF — this final step comes from general knowledge rather than the cited evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 69891.8, "error": ""}
