{"qid": "musique:b4a033f133679fb6a0a2351544933c5537546d947ad50c8f", "question": "Which two features were played up the person who had the biggest net worth in 2017?", "answers": ["ease of use and enhanced support for Plug and Play"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, **Bill Gates** was named the richest man in the world in 2017 — marking his fourth consecutive year at the top of the Forbes list of the world's billionaires[1]. In that year, there was a record of 2,043 people on the list, with a total combined net worth of US$7.67 trillion[1].\n\nHowever, I do not have sufficient retrieved evidence to determine which two \"features\" were played up by him, as the available search results did not return information connecting Bill Gates to any specific features (e.g., films or media appearances). If you could clarify what is meant by \"features\" here, I'd be happy to help further.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 72554.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:59f0697aa3d51324795d9f102b93756a0f7bc68f5ebf4922", "question": "What was the story of the character from The Bourne Deception based on?", "answers": ["the novel of the same name by Robert Ludlum", "Robert Ludlum"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **The Bourne Deception** is a novel by Eric Van Lustbader — the seventh book in the Jason Bourne series originally created by Robert Ludlum, released on June 9, 2009, and Lustbader's fourth Bourne novel[3]. So the character's story is rooted in the Jason Bourne novel series begun by Robert Ludlum, which this book continues.\n- In terms of its plot, the novel picks up where *The Bourne Sanction* left off: Jason Bourne is ambushed and badly wounded in Bali, fakes his death, takes a new identity, and sets out to discover who tried to assassinate him — a hunt that becomes entangled with the downing of an American airliner over Egypt and a global conspiracy involving the NSA and a mercenary outfit called Black River[5].\n\nNote: The knowledge base did not contain any evidence indicating the character's story was based on a real person or real event, so I can only confirm it is based on/continues Robert Ludlum's Jason Bourne novel series as described above.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 81311.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:0efe9b8bd5ade04ff3030e3d944b21b0ac4aa9527e17bde6", "question": "What year did the company that published Starship Command end?", "answers": ["1986"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**1986.**\n\nStarship Command was published by **Acornsoft** — it was released in 1983 for the BBC Micro and Acorn Electron [1]. According to the evidence, \"Acornsoft ceased to operate as a separate company upon the departure of David Johnson-Davies in January **1986**\" [10], after which Acorn Computers used the Acornsoft name on its own office software releases.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 48423.6, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:8bfb0eda3aaa2bad57532c81e874d3a974843ffd3ef0778c", "question": "How many times did plague occur in the place where Bajazet's composer was born?", "answers": ["22"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved information, here is what I can establish:\n\n**Step 1 – Composer:** The topic map identifies *Bajazet* as a pasticcio opera by **Antonio Vivaldi** (with contributions from others), first performed in Venice[1]. Vivaldi was born in **Venice**, Italy.\n\n**Step 2 – Plague occurrences in that place:** The knowledge base documents I could retrieve on plague (the \"Black Death\" article) describe recurrences at the level of Europe and other cities — e.g., plague being present somewhere in Europe every year between 1346 and 1671, repeated strikes on Algiers, over fifty epidemics in Cairo, thirty-seven epidemics in Constantinople between 1701–1750, etc.[6][7] However, these retrieved passages do **not** contain any specific count of how many times plague occurred in **Venice** itself.\n\nTherefore, while I can trace the chain — *Bajazet* → Antonio Vivaldi → born in Venice — I do not have verified evidence in the retrieved sources stating the exact number of plague occurrences in Venice, so I cannot reliably give a number. Based on the available evidence, I do not know the precise count.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 118820.5, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:027361f7300c0643dc5b5eb887d180e4917f12f6c1418ccb", "question": "Who is the spouse of the child of Peter Andreas Heiberg?", "answers": ["Johanne Luise Heiberg"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n- **Child of Peter Andreas Heiberg:** Johan Ludvig Heiberg (14 December 1791 – 25 August 1860), the Danish poet, playwright, and literary critic, who was the son of political writer Peter Andreas Heiberg (1758–1841)[1].\n\nRegarding the second step of the question — Johan Ludvig Heiberg's spouse — the retrieved evidence does not include a passage naming whom he married, so I cannot confirm his spouse's identity from the knowledge base documents returned here. (Widely known biographical sources indicate he married the celebrated Danish actress Johanne Luise Heiberg, née Pätges, but this fact was not verified in the retrieved context.)", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 94194.1, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:011cb0e253041c568db1d0b01a34fa26ff951bbbdebbd887", "question": "What character in A League of their Own was played by the actress who played Thelma in the movie Thelma and Louise?", "answers": ["Dottie Hinson"], "status": "ok", "correct": true, "answer": "**Dottie Hinson** — the star catcher of the Rockford Peaches.\n\nThe reasoning chain:\n1. In *Thelma & Louise*, the role of Thelma was played by **Geena Davis**.\n2. That same year, Geena Davis also starred in *A League of Their Own* (1992), the Penny Marshall sports comedy-drama about the All-American Girls Professional Baseball League, alongside Tom Hanks, Madonna, Lori Petty, and Rosie O'Donnell[6][10].\n\nIn that film, Geena Davis portrayed Dottie Hinson, the team's talented catcher and one of the central characters of the movie.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 62326.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:e1be2d43c39ecba4174621ae6f571ae04eac92cfd209b786", "question": "What undertaking included the person who discovered that the number of protons in each element's atoms is unique?", "answers": ["Manhattan Project"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence, the person in question is **Henry Moseley**: he showed that the atomic number Z of an element equals its positive nuclear charge, and after James Chadwick's 1932 discovery of the neutron it became clear that Z is identical to the number of protons in the nucleus — meaning each element's atoms have a unique proton count[3]. His work (Moseley's law) is treated as part of the broader account of the discovery of the neutron[1][2].\n\nAs for the undertaking that included Moseley himself: the retrieved excerpts cover his atomic-number work but do not explicitly document his participation in any undertaking. From general historical knowledge, that undertaking was **World War I** — Moseley enlisted in the British Army and was killed in action at Gallipoli in 1915 — but please note this second step was not directly confirmed by the knowledge-base evidence above.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 227783.7, "error": ""}
{"qid": "musique:a4c716e09434218a16bdfed465322e369606bd38f23d815a", "question": "Who wrote a book about growing up in the same nationality as the man who produced The Wild Women of Chastity Gulch?", "answers": ["Min Zhou", "Carl L. Bankston"], "status": "ok", "correct": false, "answer": "Based on the retrieved evidence:\n\n**Answer: Russell Baker**\n\nHere's the reasoning chain:\n\n1. **Hop 1 – The producer:** *The Wild Women of Chastity Gulch* (1982) was **executive produced by Aaron Spelling**[3]. It's described as a 1982 **American** made-for-television Western romantic comedy film directed by Philip Leacock, premiering on ABC on October 31, 1982[3].\n\n2. **Hop 2 – The nationality connection:** Aaron Spelling was **American**, consistent with the film being an American television production[3].\n\n3. **Hop 3 – The book author:** The American author who wrote a well-known book about growing up is **Russell Baker**, whose Pulitzer Prize–winning memoir literally titled *Growing Up* recounts his childhood in America — matching Aaron Spelling's American nationality.\n\nSo, **Russell Baker** wrote the book (*Growing Up*) while sharing the same nationality (American) as Aaron Spelling, the executive producer of *The Wild Women of Chastity Gulch*[3].\n\nPlease note: due to reaching the retrieval limit, only the first hop (the film's producer and nationality) could be fully verified against the knowledge base; the identification of Russell Baker as the author of *Growing Up* relies on general knowledge rather than retrieved evidence.", "tool_calls": ["kb_wiki_lookup", "knowledge_base_search"], "latency_ms": 89642.2, "error": ""}
